WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.444 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:04.920 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:16.250 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.397 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: On
bounded rationality,

00:00:18.397 --> 00:00:20.480 align:middle line:84%
I told you there are several
different ways people

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.770 align:middle line:84%
do boundedly rational
models last class.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.920 align:middle line:84%
And the one I
emphasized was this sort

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.690 align:middle line:84%
of psychology and
economics style,

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.960 align:middle line:84%
where you take one of these
rationality-like models

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.990 align:middle line:84%
you see in behavioral
economics classes,

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.720 align:middle line:84%
and you think about what happens
when consumers behave like that

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.600 align:middle line:90%
and firms exploit them.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.120 align:middle line:84%
But we say, to motivate
the rule of thumb approach,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.760 align:middle line:84%
I guess one philosophical
question of like, why is it

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.660 align:middle line:84%
we ever even do economics with
rational models to begin with?

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.020 align:middle line:84%
When we have-- you write
down some rational model,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.000 align:middle line:84%
and then you have some
very complicated--

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.167 align:middle line:84%
if you're going to publish
a paper in a top journal,

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:57.470 align:middle line:84%
you've got very complicated
mathematical argument

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.960 align:middle line:84%
of eight pages of calculations
proving what happens

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.560 align:middle line:90%
under this set of assumptions.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.709 align:middle line:84%
And then you say, therefore
this happens in this industry.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:07.100 align:middle line:84%
And your question is
like, why do we believe

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.390 align:middle line:90%
that in the first place?

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:09.770 align:middle line:84%
There's some parts
of rationality

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.478 align:middle line:84%
that we really do
believe, that if people

00:01:11.478 --> 00:01:14.020 align:middle line:84%
can do something that's better
for themselves, they'll do it.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.390 align:middle line:90%
And they have some preferences.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.980 align:middle line:84%
They have some goals
they're trying to achieve.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.110 align:middle line:84%
You can't keep fooling
them over and over again.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:21.700 align:middle line:90%
They will catch on to things.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.450 align:middle line:84%
But the full
rationality, I think,

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.020 align:middle line:90%
we don't really believe it.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.260 align:middle line:84%
And the same with these
behavioral models.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.720 align:middle line:84%
Like, OK, we believe
people are short sighted,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.420 align:middle line:84%
but do we really believe
the beta, beta hat,

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.320 align:middle line:90%
delta, quasi-hyperbolic model?

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.180 align:middle line:84%
Why not any other
functional form?

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.230 align:middle line:84%
And if there are people
are being fooled,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.715 align:middle line:84%
is that going to keep working
on them in that model,

00:01:42.715 --> 00:01:45.090 align:middle line:84%
or are they going to change
the way they behave when they

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.100 align:middle line:84%
realize that their life
is going badly because

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.220 align:middle line:90%
of something that they do?

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.120 align:middle line:84%
And are they going to
introspect and change it?

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.810 align:middle line:84%
And so, I mean, I
think one answer

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.840 align:middle line:84%
to why we do that is it does
prevent you from cheating.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.440 align:middle line:84%
It prevents you from just
assuming people do this

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.880 align:middle line:84%
and then getting your
conclusion right away.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.120 align:middle line:84%
But you can still cheat
with rational modeling.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.070 align:middle line:84%
And you just, when you're
writing down the preferences

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.300 align:middle line:84%
and you're writing
down the cost functions

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.310 align:middle line:84%
and you're writing
down the other things

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.800 align:middle line:84%
and the information, you slip
in a lot of extra assumptions

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.760 align:middle line:84%
that, in some sense, let you
get to your conclusion anyway.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.110 align:middle line:84%
And so then maybe,
sociology of economics,

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.070 align:middle line:84%
one of the reasons we do that
is because to publish a paper,

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.350 align:middle line:84%
you need to have eight
pages of complicated-seeming

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.810 align:middle line:84%
complications to show that
you've accomplished something

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.850 align:middle line:84%
hard in your paper,
and then that

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.500 align:middle line:90%
rationality lets you do that.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:37.250 align:middle line:84%
But then the question is,
is it really a good model

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.680 align:middle line:84%
if it takes a lot of pages
of complicated calculations

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:40.770 align:middle line:90%
to find out what happens?

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.312 align:middle line:84%
Because that's when
we don't really--

00:02:42.312 --> 00:02:44.630 align:middle line:84%
all the minor things that
depart from rationality

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.367 align:middle line:84%
don't matter or
could have an effect.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:48.950 align:middle line:84%
And so what the
rule-of-thumb approach

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:53.627 align:middle line:84%
tends to do is say that,
rather than writing down

00:02:53.627 --> 00:02:56.210 align:middle line:84%
a rational model and sticking
with it, even though it's really

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.160 align:middle line:84%
hard to solve and we
may not believe it,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.310 align:middle line:84%
why don't we just write
down a model that we think

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.250 align:middle line:84%
is more like what-- we think is
kind of like what consumers do,

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.310 align:middle line:84%
and we're happy to buy that
as an approximation that's

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.540 align:middle line:90%
easy to solve.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.630 align:middle line:84%
And so instead of thinking of
the assumptions on information

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:14.150 align:middle line:84%
and costs and
whatever and epsilon

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.630 align:middle line:84%
ijk as being logit
distributed, let's

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.052 align:middle line:84%
just go straight
to the behavior,

00:03:19.052 --> 00:03:21.260 align:middle line:84%
write down a behavior that
makes the model tractable,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.610 align:middle line:84%
and let us describe
what happens.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.690 align:middle line:90%
So I'll start with an example.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:34.050 align:middle line:84%
Smallwood and Conlisk is one of
my favorite classic economics

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:38.820 align:middle line:84%
papers that didn't get
taught when I took IO.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.220 align:middle line:84%
But Smallwood and
Conlisk start with--

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:46.590 align:middle line:84%
they have a question
they're starting from of,

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.570 align:middle line:84%
are superior products
going to win out

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:49.830 align:middle line:90%
in the long run in the market?

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:53.790 align:middle line:84%
And do superior products take
over markets and outcompete

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.683 align:middle line:84%
inferior products
in a world where

00:03:55.683 --> 00:03:57.600 align:middle line:84%
people don't know the
quality of every product

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.670 align:middle line:90%
when it's first invented?

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.720 align:middle line:84%
And so they use the example of,
suppose you have someone buying

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:04.523 align:middle line:90%
a box of cereal.

00:04:04.523 --> 00:04:06.690 align:middle line:84%
And they're standing here
looking at the supermarket

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.870 align:middle line:84%
shelf, and they're trying
to decide what to buy.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.060 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, they've only
tasted some small fraction

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:12.870 align:middle line:90%
of those cereals.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.412 align:middle line:84%
And so they have to
go there and look

00:04:14.412 --> 00:04:16.290 align:middle line:84%
at the prices of
some subset of them

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:20.519 align:middle line:84%
and think about how much they're
going to like them and will

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:21.670 align:middle line:90%
they like it or not.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:24.040 align:middle line:84%
If I buy it, what's the
probability I'll like it?

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.677 align:middle line:84%
Will I like it enough that
I'll want to buy it again?

00:04:26.677 --> 00:04:28.260 align:middle line:84%
And then how much
extra utility will I

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.052 align:middle line:84%
get from the future
purchase of that cereal

00:04:30.052 --> 00:04:31.990 align:middle line:84%
now that I have all
the extra information?

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.782 align:middle line:84%
And they said, well,
what's even much worse

00:04:33.782 --> 00:04:36.510 align:middle line:84%
is imagining that, when you're
going to buy the box of cereal,

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.150 align:middle line:84%
you're just walking
up to the shelf,

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:44.058 align:middle line:84%
and you see that person in front
of you buying a box of cereal.

00:04:44.058 --> 00:04:45.600 align:middle line:84%
And then what are
you supposed to do?

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.475 align:middle line:84%
You're supposed to say,
OK, well, that person

00:04:47.475 --> 00:04:48.730 align:middle line:90%
is buying this box of cereal.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:50.790 align:middle line:84%
If they bought
that box of cereal,

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.570 align:middle line:90%
they must like that cereal.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.870 align:middle line:84%
And so if they like that
cereal and then our epsilon--

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:59.215 align:middle line:84%
these things are-- there's
a xi j and an epsilon ijk.

00:04:59.215 --> 00:05:01.590 align:middle line:84%
The xi j must be high for that
cereal conditional on that

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:02.830 align:middle line:90%
person buying it.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.560 align:middle line:84%
But then you think,
well, wait, no,

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.260 align:middle line:84%
this person might be buying
it because he's tasted before,

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.000 align:middle line:84%
or that person
might be buying it

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.168 align:middle line:84%
because, two days earlier,
that person was sitting

00:05:14.168 --> 00:05:15.960 align:middle line:84%
on their couch at home,
and there was a TV,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.480 align:middle line:84%
and they were watching a TV,
and there was a commercial.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.960 align:middle line:84%
And they saw the TV
commercial for that cereal.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.440 align:middle line:84%
But if they did see
the TV commercial,

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.300 align:middle line:84%
what's the signaling value of
the TV commercial that they saw?

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.540 align:middle line:84%
Because the firm wouldn't
have advertised the cereal

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.832 align:middle line:84%
unless it knew people were
going to repeat purchase it.

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:35.680 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I have to have
a prior over the cost

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.290 align:middle line:84%
of that cereal and the reasons
why the TV commercial would

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:39.490 align:middle line:90%
have been on.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.080 align:middle line:84%
But then again,
maybe this person

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.810 align:middle line:84%
is buying it because the person
who was two people before them

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.600 align:middle line:84%
in the aisle bought it and they
saw the person before take it

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.790 align:middle line:84%
off that aisle, or maybe
they're just buying it

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:50.805 align:middle line:90%
because it's at eye level.

00:05:50.805 --> 00:05:52.180 align:middle line:84%
But if it's at
eye level, there's

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.287 align:middle line:84%
this signaling problem of,
why is it at eye level?

00:05:56.287 --> 00:05:58.120 align:middle line:84%
Is it eye level because
it's really popular,

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.720 align:middle line:84%
or is it at eye level because
the firm paid the store

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:02.750 align:middle line:90%
to put it at eye level?

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:04.420 align:middle line:84%
And would they have paid more
or less to put it at eye level

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.890 align:middle line:90%
if it was good or bad?

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:08.110 align:middle line:84%
But especially if this
person saw the person before,

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:11.950 align:middle line:84%
it's almost like I need
this prior over all possible

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:15.070 align:middle line:84%
purchases by every person
since the invention of cereal

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:18.700 align:middle line:84%
in the 19th century and who saw
what and what they did and what

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.210 align:middle line:90%
that did to the popularity.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:23.860 align:middle line:84%
And then I've got to have that
prior over that enormous network

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.750 align:middle line:84%
of a century's worth
of cereal purchases,

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:28.060 align:middle line:84%
and then invert it
and figure out, now

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:29.870 align:middle line:84%
what's my posterior
on that cereal box?

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:33.340 align:middle line:84%
And Smallwood and Conlisk's
view is that this is insane.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.740 align:middle line:90%
This is not what people do.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:35.540 align:middle line:90%
People walk up.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:37.080 align:middle line:90%
They look at the cereal box.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.940 align:middle line:84%
They're buying 50 different
things at the supermarket.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:40.010 align:middle line:84%
Most of the time,
they just take off

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.030 align:middle line:84%
the shelf whatever they
normally take off the shelf.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.697 align:middle line:84%
And then every once in a while,
they've got a little extra time,

00:06:44.697 --> 00:06:46.640 align:middle line:84%
and they put a
little bit of time

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.650 align:middle line:84%
into thinking,
which one do I buy?

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:53.660 align:middle line:84%
And so why don't we think
about consumers more like that

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:58.290 align:middle line:84%
and ask about, then
what would happen?

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:00.090 align:middle line:84%
Would good cereal
brands take over,

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.320 align:middle line:84%
or do we think that Cheerios
is really popular even

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:08.090 align:middle line:84%
though Cheerios isn't very
good or whatever it is?

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:11.330 align:middle line:84%
So here's what Smallwood
and Conlisk did

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.000 align:middle line:84%
and this idea of just
trying to write down--

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.150 align:middle line:84%
let's write down a dynamic model
that seems reasonable to us

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:19.970 align:middle line:84%
and talk about, what's
the long-run implications

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:22.430 align:middle line:84%
in terms of, do superior
brands take over

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:26.810 align:middle line:84%
when consumers are fairly
naive in their shopping?

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:29.630 align:middle line:84%
And I think they very much
do believe that consumers

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:35.360 align:middle line:84%
are naive when they shop
and that this is a better

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.200 align:middle line:90%
model than the rational one.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.750 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, here's their model.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.240 align:middle line:90%
Products differ in quality.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.170 align:middle line:84%
A consumer of product
k has a bad experience,

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:47.552 align:middle line:84%
which they'll call a
breakdown with probability bk.

00:07:47.552 --> 00:07:49.760 align:middle line:84%
So if you're talking about
people buying light bulbs,

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.385 align:middle line:84%
that could be literally
the probability

00:07:51.385 --> 00:07:54.200 align:middle line:84%
that the light bulb
burns out in any given--

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:55.910 align:middle line:90%
between any given shopping trip.

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:58.497 align:middle line:84%
If it's a cereal, it just could
be that every once in a while,

00:07:58.497 --> 00:08:00.080 align:middle line:84%
you're eating your
cereal, and it just

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.370 align:middle line:84%
occurs to you in the
morning, you know,

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:02.580 align:middle line:90%
this really isn't all that good.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.163 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I should buy
a different cereal.

00:08:04.163 --> 00:08:06.560 align:middle line:84%
But products differ
in the probability

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.310 align:middle line:90%
of giving a bad experience.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:09.957 align:middle line:84%
And so the highest
quality products

00:08:09.957 --> 00:08:11.290 align:middle line:90%
are the ones with the lowest bk.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:14.220 align:middle line:90%


00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:18.690 align:middle line:84%
What consumers do is they always
continue to buy the same product

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:22.980 align:middle line:84%
they bought previously until
one of these breakdowns occur.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:26.430 align:middle line:84%
When the breakdown
occurs, they decide

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:29.670 align:middle line:84%
to buy product k
with some probability

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:33.360 align:middle line:84%
mk of t raised to the
sigma power, where mk of t

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.549 align:middle line:84%
is the market share
of the product.

00:08:35.549 --> 00:08:38.159 align:middle line:84%
So they just buy it
with some probability

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:41.320 align:middle line:84%
that's related to the
product's current market share.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:41.820 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:45.000 align:middle line:90%
So one example, sigma equals 0.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.570 align:middle line:84%
Sigma equals 0 would just
be when a breakdown occurs,

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:52.620 align:middle line:84%
I just buy a product in
proportion to its popularity.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.185 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that the
supermarket stock shelves

00:08:55.185 --> 00:08:57.180 align:middle line:84%
so that the number of
boxes of each cereal

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:00.455 align:middle line:84%
is related to how
many people buy it,

00:09:00.455 --> 00:09:01.830 align:middle line:84%
and therefore I
just pick one off

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:03.860 align:middle line:90%
the shelf entirely at random.

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:05.550 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:06.270 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:09.300 align:middle line:84%
Sigma 0 would be that there's
one box of each cereal.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.430 align:middle line:84%
I pick the box of the
cereal off at random

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:13.920 align:middle line:90%
when there's one box of each.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:21.910 align:middle line:84%
Sigma equals 1 is, I buy it in
proportion to the market share.

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:26.070 align:middle line:84%
So that could be they
stock more boxes of--

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:29.070 align:middle line:84%
the more popular cereals
get more shelf space,

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:30.730 align:middle line:84%
and you pick according
to shelf space.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:33.810 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be that I
watch the person before me,

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:36.990 align:middle line:84%
I see them pick off a
cereal, and I say, OK,

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.450 align:middle line:84%
that person likes it,
maybe I will try it.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:43.290 align:middle line:84%
So that would be a
sigma equals 1 model.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:47.820 align:middle line:84%
A larger sigma could be
some explicit reasoning

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.530 align:middle line:84%
about popularity,
saying, this one

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:51.330 align:middle line:84%
seems to be the most
popular, maybe I

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:54.150 align:middle line:84%
will try that, that I think
popularity is probably

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:57.180 align:middle line:84%
correlated with fewer
breakdowns, therefore,

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.330 align:middle line:90%
I will buy a more popular brand.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:02.110 align:middle line:84%
And so, sigma going
to infinity would

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:06.900 align:middle line:84%
be, you buy the most popular
brand with probability 1.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:09.840 align:middle line:84%
And then the main result
of Smallwood and Conlisk

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.010 align:middle line:84%
is that, with fairly
naive consumers,

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.133 align:middle line:84%
social learning
can work very well.

00:10:16.133 --> 00:10:17.550 align:middle line:84%
And what it has
to be is they have

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:20.630 align:middle line:84%
to use the right amount
of weight on popularity.

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:23.940 align:middle line:84%
So the theorem is,
if sigma equals

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.650 align:middle line:84%
1, the most popular product
dominates in the t goes

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:29.770 align:middle line:90%
to infinity limit.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:33.570 align:middle line:84%
That is, the product that
has the smallest bk--

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:39.390 align:middle line:90%
so it's that mk of t goes to 1.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:42.840 align:middle line:84%
So limit as t goes to
infinity of mk of t

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:54.000 align:middle line:84%
goes to 1 for k equals
the argmin of bk.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.260 align:middle line:84%
The product that has the
lowest breakdown probability

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:00.940 align:middle line:84%
has its market share converge
to 1 in the long run.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:03.130 align:middle line:90%
So the best product takes over.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:05.350 align:middle line:90%
All other products die out.

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:08.620 align:middle line:84%
And that happens when
people put the sigma equals

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.880 align:middle line:90%
1 weight on probability.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:13.000 align:middle line:90%
So they're not really explicit.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.150 align:middle line:84%
It's as if you just do the
"watch one person in front

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:18.710 align:middle line:90%
of you" model.

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:21.220 align:middle line:84%
So it's using very minimal
information about probability.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.560 align:middle line:90%
That converges to 1.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:28.640 align:middle line:84%
When sigma is less than
1, we converge to a state

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.770 align:middle line:90%
with all k products active.

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:34.230 align:middle line:84%
So each product has then a
market share between 0 and 1

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.270 align:middle line:90%
in the long run.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:38.010 align:middle line:84%
Inferior products stay
alive in the market.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:39.990 align:middle line:84%
Superior products have
bigger market shares.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:42.020 align:middle line:90%
They don't go to 1.

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:46.220 align:middle line:84%
And then, anything like
the sigma equals infinity,

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:47.870 align:middle line:84%
but any sigma
greater than 1 where

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:49.700 align:middle line:84%
you pick the most
popular product,

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:52.890 align:middle line:84%
then inferior products can
come to dominate the market,

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:55.580 align:middle line:84%
where if you just get
some initial popularity,

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:57.920 align:middle line:84%
you're initially
popular, people buy you,

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:00.800 align:middle line:84%
your popularity just goes
to 1, and superior products

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.332 align:middle line:84%
can't outcompete you
because you're more popular.

00:12:03.332 --> 00:12:04.790 align:middle line:84%
So Smallwood and
Conlisk's argument

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:08.180 align:middle line:84%
was that markets
can select things,

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:12.330 align:middle line:84%
but there's this
awkward part of it,

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.000 align:middle line:84%
that consumers have to be
fairly naive to get the market

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:16.180 align:middle line:90%
to select the best products.

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:19.062 align:middle line:84%
They have to put fairly
little weight on popularity.

00:12:19.062 --> 00:12:20.520 align:middle line:84%
They put some weight
on popularity,

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:22.860 align:middle line:84%
but it's got to be the
equivalent to the sigma equals

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:27.220 align:middle line:84%
1 weight on popularity to get
social learning to work out.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:27.720 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.137 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so I'm just going to
then just talk about a couple

00:12:30.137 --> 00:12:36.280 align:middle line:84%
of other papers that have built
on this and done other things.

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:43.590 align:middle line:84%
So I have a paper
with Drew, 1995,

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:47.310 align:middle line:84%
where we try to take the
Smallwood and Conlisk idea

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:51.900 align:middle line:84%
and make it a bit more like
a classic economics utility

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:55.140 align:middle line:90%
framework.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:59.430 align:middle line:84%
So here now, when consumer
i's consume products,

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:02.190 align:middle line:84%
they receive utility,
and the utility

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:06.330 align:middle line:84%
you get from consuming a product
is a product specific mean.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:09.040 align:middle line:84%
This is like the
xi j or whatever.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:11.890 align:middle line:84%
So you get a product
specific mean utility.

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:15.070 align:middle line:84%
And you get an
epsilon ikt, which

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:17.616 align:middle line:84%
is your own
idiosyncratic preference.

00:13:17.616 --> 00:13:20.450 align:middle line:90%


00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:25.070 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
assume that the utility

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:28.220 align:middle line:84%
difference between product
1 and probability 2

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:32.270 align:middle line:84%
is a random variable, where
sometimes product 1 is better,

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:34.230 align:middle line:84%
or sometimes
product 2 is better.

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:36.020 align:middle line:84%
So this is something
about these products

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:40.870 align:middle line:84%
that there is a period-specific
choice to them that makes one

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:42.120 align:middle line:90%
of them better than the other.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:44.620 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, it could be
that these two products are not

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:46.220 align:middle line:90%
actually cereal boxes.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:49.080 align:middle line:84%
It could be those two products
are ways to drive to work.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:50.760 align:middle line:84%
Do I drive to work
on the Mass Pike?

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:52.740 align:middle line:84%
Do I drive to work
on Storrow Drive?

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:57.140 align:middle line:84%
And every day, one of those two
ways to drive to work is better,

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:00.710 align:middle line:90%
but it changes from day to day.

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:02.180 align:middle line:84%
It could be that
these are products

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:05.450 align:middle line:84%
that you're buying, where
there are two restaurants,

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:08.360 align:middle line:84%
and because the food quality
varies from day to day

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:10.070 align:middle line:84%
due to the quality
of the ingredients

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:14.313 align:middle line:84%
or the chef that's working,
on some days shop A,

00:14:14.313 --> 00:14:15.980 align:middle line:84%
the food is better
than shop B, and then

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:19.380 align:middle line:90%
some days shop B is better.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:21.388 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be that
these are different--

00:14:21.388 --> 00:14:23.430 align:middle line:84%
you're buying from different
insurance companies.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:26.010 align:middle line:84%
And if there's a bad
event, then one of them

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:27.302 align:middle line:90%
was better to have bought from.

00:14:27.302 --> 00:14:29.343 align:middle line:84%
And if there was no bad
event, then the other one

00:14:29.343 --> 00:14:30.610 align:middle line:90%
is better to have bought from.

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:33.690 align:middle line:84%
So we have something that makes
the quality of the two products

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:36.540 align:middle line:90%
differ.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:39.330 align:middle line:84%
Product 1 is better
with probability p.

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:43.470 align:middle line:84%
So you would like
to buy the product

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.750 align:middle line:84%
1 if p is bigger than a
half and buy product 2 if p

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:47.660 align:middle line:90%
is less than a half.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:53.270 align:middle line:84%
And then we have Smallwood
and Conlisk-like consumers.

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:54.560 align:middle line:90%
We don't have breakdowns.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:58.010 align:middle line:84%
So the inertia part is,
consumers buy the same product

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:01.790 align:middle line:84%
they bought previously with
probability 1 minus alpha.

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:04.490 align:middle line:84%
And with probability
alpha, they try

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:06.680 align:middle line:84%
to decide what-- they
consider changing

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:08.870 align:middle line:84%
and think about what
they should buy.

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:11.450 align:middle line:84%
And when they consider
changing, what they do

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:18.470 align:middle line:84%
is ask N of their friends about
their most recent experience.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:26.180 align:middle line:90%
I form u1t bar to be the sum--

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:30.800 align:middle line:90%
or I form u1t bar and u2t bar.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:33.590 align:middle line:84%
This is the average of the
utilities received by all

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:35.330 align:middle line:90%
my friends who use product 1.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:37.580 align:middle line:84%
This is the average of the
utilities of all my friends

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:38.840 align:middle line:90%
who use product 2.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:42.050 align:middle line:84%
And then I pick whichever one
has the better average when

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:44.030 align:middle line:90%
I've surveyed them.

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:46.820 align:middle line:90%
Again, I asked the friends who--

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:50.660 align:middle line:84%
I asked the friends who took
Storrow Drive which is better

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:51.835 align:middle line:90%
for them, and vice versa.

00:15:51.835 --> 00:15:55.100 align:middle line:90%


00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:57.530 align:middle line:84%
And then if all friends--
if everyone I ask

00:15:57.530 --> 00:15:59.910 align:middle line:84%
bought the same product,
then I just buy that one.

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:03.590 align:middle line:84%
So I take an average of 0
over 0 is negative infinity,

00:16:03.590 --> 00:16:05.240 align:middle line:90%
so I don't buy that one.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:09.140 align:middle line:84%
And so the observation
is, in this model,

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:12.050 align:middle line:84%
again, we can sometimes
get optimal social learning

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:15.260 align:middle line:84%
from just asking
friends about products.

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:19.340 align:middle line:84%
Typically, this occurs
when N is fairly small.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:24.710 align:middle line:84%
So this is a graph showing,
as a function of alpha, what's

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:28.850 align:middle line:90%
the range of Ns for which--

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:31.970 align:middle line:84%
what's the range of Ns
for which this model gives

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:33.690 align:middle line:90%
efficient social learning?

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:35.900 align:middle line:84%
And this graph seems
to be showing us

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:39.530 align:middle line:84%
that if alpha is close to 1,
almost everybody is asking

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:40.770 align:middle line:90%
their friends every period.

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:42.690 align:middle line:84%
Then you need to be
asking three, four, five,

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:44.070 align:middle line:90%
or six friends.

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:45.800 align:middle line:84%
Most of the time,
when alpha is small,

00:16:45.800 --> 00:16:48.710 align:middle line:84%
this model with the "ask
one friend what they bought"

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:49.692 align:middle line:90%
works well.

00:16:49.692 --> 00:16:51.900 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, asking
one friend what they bought,

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:53.540 align:middle line:84%
that's a lot like
Smallwood and Conlisk

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:56.580 align:middle line:84%
because that's like choosing
in proportion to market share.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:59.820 align:middle line:84%
But then when alpha is
bigger, what you want to do

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:01.680 align:middle line:90%
is gather more information.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:05.670 align:middle line:84%
And the more information helps
you find the better products.

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:12.740 align:middle line:84%
And again, what's going on here
is that there's this more--

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:16.000 align:middle line:84%
better products do have
higher mean utilities.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:20.210 align:middle line:84%
And then there's also some
effect of popularity weighting

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:22.532 align:middle line:84%
in that if something
is more popular,

00:17:22.532 --> 00:17:23.990 align:middle line:84%
you're more likely
to hear about it

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:26.130 align:middle line:84%
and more likely to not
hear about the popularity.

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:29.300 align:middle line:84%
And the combination
of those two things

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:32.570 align:middle line:84%
can give us social learning
but doesn't give us social

00:17:32.570 --> 00:17:35.970 align:middle line:90%
learning if N is too big.

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:39.673 align:middle line:84%
And then one other
paper along these lines.

00:17:39.673 --> 00:17:42.360 align:middle line:90%


00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:46.530 align:middle line:84%
Ran Spiegler has a paper,
another follow-on paper,

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:51.420 align:middle line:84%
he calls "The Market for
Quacks," which is the--

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.840 align:middle line:84%
my paper with Drew, we were
following Smallwood and Conlisk

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:57.540 align:middle line:84%
in not having any prices
at all in the model,

00:17:57.540 --> 00:17:59.827 align:middle line:84%
which works well for
the people learning

00:17:59.827 --> 00:18:02.160 align:middle line:84%
which way to drive to work
or learning things like that.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:06.480 align:middle line:84%
Doesn't work as well
necessarily for buying products.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:10.230 align:middle line:84%
Spiegler thinks about what
happens in word-of-mouth models

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:12.720 align:middle line:90%
if you let the firms set prices.

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:14.580 align:middle line:84%
So he calls it "The
Market for Quacks"

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:16.570 align:middle line:84%
because he's thinking
about the application--

00:18:16.570 --> 00:18:21.750 align:middle line:84%
his application he's thinking
about is people selling the many

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:25.830 align:middle line:84%
medications and health things or
whatever that you see advertised

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:29.902 align:middle line:84%
online that are probably no
better than not buying some kind

00:18:29.902 --> 00:18:31.860 align:middle line:84%
of health supplement
that's supposed to improve

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.780 align:middle line:90%
your whatever.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:37.420 align:middle line:84%
And so what he imagines is that
he has this model where you have

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:41.320 align:middle line:84%
this large number of products,
and whenever you consume

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:43.840 align:middle line:84%
one of these products,
with probability alpha,

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:46.870 align:middle line:84%
you do get a good outcome
after having consumed it.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:49.330 align:middle line:84%
But of course, there's an
outside option that gives you

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:53.560 align:middle line:84%
utility that costs 0, and you
also get a positive experience

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:56.080 align:middle line:84%
sometimes after not consuming
any of these products.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:59.920 align:middle line:84%
And in particular, he's got
that the outside option--

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:05.800 align:middle line:84%
there's an outside
option which also

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:09.700 align:middle line:84%
gives you a good experience with
probability alpha and costs 0.

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:11.950 align:middle line:84%
So the outside option
is actually just better

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:13.125 align:middle line:90%
than all of these products.

00:19:13.125 --> 00:19:16.790 align:middle line:90%


00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:20.090 align:middle line:84%
So what happens is, the utility
that a consumer i gets in period

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:25.460 align:middle line:84%
t if they buy product k is 1
minus pkt with probability alpha

00:19:25.460 --> 00:19:30.260 align:middle line:84%
and minus pkt with
probability 1 minus alpha.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:36.157 align:middle line:84%
And what consumers do in
each period is, again,

00:19:36.157 --> 00:19:38.240 align:middle line:84%
like Smallwood and Conlisk,
we have this continuum

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:39.090 align:middle line:90%
of consumers.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:41.250 align:middle line:84%
Some consumers are
using every product.

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:43.400 align:middle line:84%
What consumers do
in each period is,

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:47.420 align:middle line:84%
ask one user of each product
about their experience

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:49.790 align:middle line:84%
and buy the product that
would give the highest

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:53.090 align:middle line:84%
utility assuming the
same experience if it

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:54.680 align:middle line:90%
gives positive utility.

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:59.130 align:middle line:84%
So I ask-- there are
k different products.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:00.680 align:middle line:84%
I find one person
using each product.

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:02.370 align:middle line:90%
Well, how did you like it?

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:03.243 align:middle line:90%
Did it work for you?

00:20:03.243 --> 00:20:05.910 align:middle line:84%
And three people tell you that,
yeah, the product worked for me.

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:09.630 align:middle line:84%
It gave me utility 1,
but I paid this for it.

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:11.370 align:middle line:84%
Seven people tell
you I didn't like it.

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:12.410 align:middle line:90%
It gave me utility 0.

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:14.970 align:middle line:84%
And I paid money for it,
so I got negative utility.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:20.487 align:middle line:84%
And so then what consumers
do is they pick the--

00:20:20.487 --> 00:20:23.070 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, they're going to
choose among the products that

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:25.560 align:middle line:84%
gave utility 1 to their
friends the one that's

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:27.690 align:middle line:84%
offered at the lowest
price this period.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:29.430 align:middle line:84%
And if every one
of their friends

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:31.950 align:middle line:84%
got 0, got negative
utility, then they just

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:32.975 align:middle line:90%
buy the outside option.

00:20:32.975 --> 00:20:36.820 align:middle line:90%


00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:37.320 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:38.970 align:middle line:90%
What happens here?

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:42.510 align:middle line:84%
This model is--
if you think back

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:45.390 align:middle line:84%
to the Stahl model
of price dispersion,

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:48.120 align:middle line:84%
this model is going to be
kind of like that Stahl

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:53.610 align:middle line:84%
model of price dispersion,
because imagine you're a firm.

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:55.290 align:middle line:84%
You're a firm, and
you know that there's

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:56.957 align:middle line:84%
going to be some
consumers out there who

00:20:56.957 --> 00:20:59.880 align:middle line:84%
heard positive reviews
of your product

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:02.580 align:middle line:84%
and negative reviews
of every other product.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.190 align:middle line:84%
And so if they heard a positive
news about your product

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:06.690 align:middle line:84%
and negative about
everybody else's,

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.868 align:middle line:90%
you'd want to set p equals 1.

00:21:08.868 --> 00:21:10.410 align:middle line:84%
But then there can
be other consumers

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:12.660 align:middle line:84%
who heard positive things
about your product and about

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:14.077 align:middle line:90%
exactly one other product.

00:21:14.077 --> 00:21:16.410 align:middle line:84%
And so if they heard positive
things about two products,

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:19.513 align:middle line:84%
including yours, you want to be
epsilon less than the product

00:21:19.513 --> 00:21:21.180 align:middle line:84%
that they heard-- the
other product they

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:22.740 align:middle line:90%
heard good things about.

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:25.190 align:middle line:84%
And so it's kind of like
that kind of Stahl model,

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:26.940 align:middle line:84%
where there's multiple
types of consumers,

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.070 align:middle line:84%
the consumers who
heard good things only

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:32.398 align:middle line:84%
about yours, where they're
like the non-shoppers,

00:21:32.398 --> 00:21:34.440 align:middle line:84%
and the consumers who
heard good things about you

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:35.700 align:middle line:90%
and several others.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:38.830 align:middle line:84%
And there, you want to be
undercutting the other prices.

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:42.540 align:middle line:84%
So what you're going to get
is some kind of mixed strategy

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:46.020 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium, where you're
mixing over some interval,

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.630 align:middle line:84%
and you're sometimes
charging a high price

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:50.700 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes
charging a low price

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:53.970 align:middle line:84%
to try to get the people who've
heard about multiple products.

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:57.058 align:middle line:84%
It's not as extreme as that
Stahl model in that there's--

00:21:57.058 --> 00:21:59.100 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be very rare
for consumers who have

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:00.600 align:middle line:90%
heard about all N products.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:03.570 align:middle line:84%
So you don't need to be the
single lowest price product

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:04.742 align:middle line:90%
in the market to get people.

00:22:04.742 --> 00:22:07.200 align:middle line:84%
They've probably only heard
about two or three or something

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:08.170 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:11.640 align:middle line:84%
And so anyway, you'll
have some distribution--

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:17.126 align:middle line:84%
I imagine it probably looks like
this-- of what prices look like.

00:22:17.126 --> 00:22:22.210 align:middle line:84%
An interesting observation
is that in this model,

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:26.140 align:middle line:84%
price is inversely related
to product quality.

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:31.660 align:middle line:84%
So if you imagine, if
the products are all--

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:33.310 align:middle line:90%
this is common product quality.

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:36.670 align:middle line:84%
If the products are all
good and people actually

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:39.130 align:middle line:84%
have positive
experiences with them,

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:42.250 align:middle line:84%
then you as a firm
selling are going to be

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:44.620 align:middle line:84%
like, OK, if someone
heard about my--

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:47.080 align:middle line:84%
their friend had a good
experience with my product,

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:48.580 align:middle line:84%
they probably also
talked to friends

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:50.622 align:middle line:84%
who had good experiences
with the other products.

00:22:50.622 --> 00:22:53.590 align:middle line:84%
So I'm probably competing with
three or four or five or six

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:55.280 align:middle line:84%
other products for
their business.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:56.980 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, I need to
price aggressively

00:22:56.980 --> 00:22:59.830 align:middle line:84%
to be-- beat those four
or five or six products.

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:04.330 align:middle line:84%
But if the products are terrible
and there's a 1 in 100 chance

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:06.490 align:middle line:84%
that they actually
do anything, then you

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:08.370 align:middle line:84%
would think, OK,
well, if someone

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:10.120 align:middle line:84%
had a positive experience
with my product,

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:12.042 align:middle line:90%
then when they're asking--

00:23:12.042 --> 00:23:13.750 align:middle line:84%
they found a friend
who liked my product.

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:14.830 align:middle line:84%
They probably
didn't find a friend

00:23:14.830 --> 00:23:16.330 align:middle line:84%
who liked any of
the other products.

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:18.430 align:middle line:84%
So I'm probably a
monopolist, so I'm probably

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:20.480 align:middle line:90%
safe pricing close to 1.

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:25.880 align:middle line:84%
So in equilibrium, competition
between low quality products,

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:27.050 align:middle line:90%
we get high prices.

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:30.470 align:middle line:84%
Competition between high quality
products, we get low prices.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:33.200 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is that the
competition between high quality

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:36.200 align:middle line:84%
products is actually
more real competition,

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:38.540 align:middle line:84%
whereas the competition for
the low quality products

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:40.010 align:middle line:84%
is just getting
the people who have

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:44.160 align:middle line:90%
the rare mistaken impression.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:47.920 align:middle line:84%
So this is not a
fully rational model.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:54.200 align:middle line:84%
The consumers are not somehow
inverting the equilibrium price

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:56.150 align:middle line:84%
distribution or inverting
the market shares

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:59.420 align:middle line:84%
and realizing, OK,
if prices are high,

00:23:59.420 --> 00:24:01.230 align:middle line:84%
it must be that
the quality is low.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:04.407 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, I'm learning
something about--

00:24:04.407 --> 00:24:05.990 align:middle line:84%
I had a prior about
alpha, and now I'm

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:07.970 align:middle line:84%
learning something
about alpha, or I'm not

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:11.540 align:middle line:84%
updating alpha from realizing
that I'm just getting i.i.d.

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:13.910 align:middle line:84%
These are all-- these
seem to be i.i.d draws

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:15.410 align:middle line:90%
as I draw them every period.

00:24:15.410 --> 00:24:18.510 align:middle line:84%
They're just every period
taking this person liked it,

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:19.965 align:middle line:90%
therefore this is quality 1.

00:24:19.965 --> 00:24:21.090 align:middle line:90%
This person didn't like it.

00:24:21.090 --> 00:24:23.690 align:middle line:90%
These are all quality 0.

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:27.380 align:middle line:84%
But I think Spiegler's
view is that this

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:33.860 align:middle line:84%
seems like a reasonable force
and that the naive model may

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:36.830 align:middle line:84%
be a better way to think about,
that this force of competition

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:41.800 align:middle line:84%
or quality drives down
prices could be a real thing.

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:42.680 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:46.080 align:middle line:84%
Second thing I want
to do is-- and I

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:49.650 align:middle line:84%
figured I would do
something fun today

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:51.790 align:middle line:84%
in honor of being here
before the holidays.

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:54.210 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I'm going
to talk about-- this

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:58.270 align:middle line:84%
is another rule-of-thumb
paper I've written.

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:00.240 align:middle line:84%
It's not actually
really an IO paper,

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:03.180 align:middle line:84%
but I feel it's useful for
graduate students who have

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:04.860 align:middle line:90%
thought about these issues.

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:09.900 align:middle line:84%
So this is a paper thinking
about the journal refereeing

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:10.480 align:middle line:90%
process.

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:14.588 align:middle line:84%
So 6 or 7 years from now, many
of you will be professors,

00:25:14.588 --> 00:25:16.380 align:middle line:84%
and you'll be sitting
in offices somewhere,

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:18.450 align:middle line:84%
and this is what you'll
be complaining about,

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:21.120 align:middle line:84%
is the journal
refereeing process.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:26.550 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, what's very
difficult, as people find out,

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:30.300 align:middle line:84%
is somehow it's very, very hard
to publish an economics paper,

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:32.430 align:middle line:84%
and it takes years and
years and years and years.

00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:34.380 align:middle line:84%
And you try to write
about something topical,

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:36.510 align:middle line:84%
and then it takes five
years to get your paper

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:38.612 align:middle line:84%
through the journal
refereeing process.

00:25:38.612 --> 00:25:41.070 align:middle line:84%
And after five years getting
through the journal refereeing

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:43.952 align:middle line:84%
process, your
wonderfully topical thing

00:25:43.952 --> 00:25:45.910 align:middle line:84%
is now completely irrelevant
because the things

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:49.720 align:middle line:84%
you're writing about
are no longer topical.

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:52.610 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the experience--
I'm a department head.

00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:55.115 align:middle line:84%
I'm also on Promotions and
Tenure Committee for people

00:25:55.115 --> 00:25:55.990 align:middle line:90%
in other departments.

00:25:55.990 --> 00:25:58.870 align:middle line:84%
And in other social science
fields where people write books,

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:00.860 align:middle line:84%
this problem also just
appears in spades.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:03.170 align:middle line:84%
We're constantly reading
cases of, here's this person.

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:05.080 align:middle line:84%
They've been a faculty
member for five years.

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:07.692 align:middle line:84%
Their book, which was based on
their graduate student thesis,

00:26:07.692 --> 00:26:09.400 align:middle line:84%
is still not out yet
because they've just

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:12.110 align:middle line:84%
been revising their book
for the past five years.

00:26:12.110 --> 00:26:13.960 align:middle line:84%
And the book is five
years less timely

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:17.080 align:middle line:84%
than it was when they
wrote it originally.

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:20.440 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, how do I think about
why publication works this way?

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:21.440 align:middle line:90%
Here's a theory.

00:26:21.440 --> 00:26:28.330 align:middle line:84%
So in the model, papers have a
two-dimensional quality q and r.

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:33.310 align:middle line:84%
q I think of as the
fundamental idea in the paper

00:26:33.310 --> 00:26:35.830 align:middle line:84%
or the quality of the
unchangeable insight

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:37.563 align:middle line:90%
that this paper has.

00:26:37.563 --> 00:26:38.980 align:middle line:84%
The q is what you
would teach when

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:40.855 align:middle line:84%
you teach that paper in
your graduate course.

00:26:40.855 --> 00:26:42.320 align:middle line:90%
This is the paper's idea.

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:44.008 align:middle line:84%
This is how that
paper changes the way

00:26:44.008 --> 00:26:45.050 align:middle line:90%
we think about something.

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:47.970 align:middle line:84%
This was their interesting
idea they came up with.

00:26:47.970 --> 00:26:52.860 align:middle line:84%
And then the r is the
other dimensions of quality

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:55.270 align:middle line:84%
that you can improve
in a revision.

00:26:55.270 --> 00:26:58.050 align:middle line:84%
So rather than having
linear functional forms,

00:26:58.050 --> 00:27:01.090 align:middle line:84%
you can do everything with
arbitrary functional forms.

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:03.120 align:middle line:84%
You can go through
the seven most

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:06.120 align:middle line:84%
recent econometric techniques
published in Econometrica

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:09.040 align:middle line:84%
and re-estimate it using these
seven different estimators

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:11.413 align:middle line:84%
and talk about how all
those estimators do things.

00:27:11.413 --> 00:27:13.830 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, some parts of the
things we improve in revision

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:16.750 align:middle line:84%
are making us more
confident in the results.

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:18.040 align:middle line:90%
They are valuable.

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:21.032 align:middle line:84%
Many of them are just
doing everything--

00:27:21.032 --> 00:27:23.490 align:middle line:84%
addressing every potential
criticism that anyone could have

00:27:23.490 --> 00:27:27.540 align:middle line:84%
come up with and just continuing
to just make your paper harder

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:31.650 align:middle line:90%
and harder to read.

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:36.390 align:middle line:84%
So we value both aspects
of quality, the idea part

00:27:36.390 --> 00:27:39.240 align:middle line:90%
and the craftsmanship part.

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:41.880 align:middle line:84%
And referees know that
they're supposed--

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:44.250 align:middle line:84%
that there's a social
norm in the profession

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.510 align:middle line:84%
that we put alpha
weight on the q

00:27:46.510 --> 00:27:49.000 align:middle line:84%
and we put 1 minus
alpha weight on the r.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:52.040 align:middle line:84%
And they know that they're
supposed to recommend acceptance

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:56.100 align:middle line:84%
if this weighted average
of q and r is at least z.

00:27:56.100 --> 00:28:01.840 align:middle line:84%
But referees being people
who are, in general, 32

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:04.300 align:middle line:84%
years old or whatever, not
in the profession for all

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:07.783 align:middle line:84%
that long, don't necessarily
know what alpha and z are.

00:28:07.783 --> 00:28:08.950 align:middle line:90%
And they're trying to learn.

00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:10.750 align:middle line:84%
They're just trying
to be good referees

00:28:10.750 --> 00:28:12.760 align:middle line:84%
and evaluate papers
appropriately

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:14.420 align:middle line:90%
when asked to evaluate a paper.

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:16.960 align:middle line:84%
And so at time t, the
profession ends up

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:19.600 align:middle line:84%
using these weights,
alpha t and zt.

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:21.820 align:middle line:84%
And all the referees
in the profession

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:24.070 align:middle line:84%
are just trying to
update all the time

00:28:24.070 --> 00:28:26.860 align:middle line:84%
and learn, what is alpha t,
what's the weight on these two,

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:28.780 align:middle line:84%
and what's the quality
threshold where I'm

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:30.090 align:middle line:90%
supposed to say yes versus no?

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:32.830 align:middle line:90%


00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:35.530 align:middle line:84%
I assume that authors
rationally divide their time

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:38.260 align:middle line:84%
between producing the
two types of quality.

00:28:38.260 --> 00:28:41.320 align:middle line:84%
They start by picking
some amount of time tq.

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:44.410 align:middle line:84%
So 1 is the total amount
of time you have available.

00:28:44.410 --> 00:28:50.230 align:middle line:84%
You start by spending some time
tq in 0,1 to get a random draw

00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:51.760 align:middle line:90%
on an idea.

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:54.010 align:middle line:84%
And at the end of
that time tq that you

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:57.880 align:middle line:84%
spent developing random
draws on the ideas,

00:28:57.880 --> 00:29:01.250 align:middle line:84%
then you get an
idea of quality q.

00:29:01.250 --> 00:29:05.780 align:middle line:84%
And there's some CDF F of q
given tq that's increasing.

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:09.050 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, it could be
that just q is uniform on 0

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:10.450 align:middle line:90%
to tq, is one.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:14.480 align:middle line:90%


00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:20.410 align:middle line:84%
But you could also make it
normal mean tq or whatever.

00:29:20.410 --> 00:29:24.257 align:middle line:84%
And then after
you get your idea,

00:29:24.257 --> 00:29:25.840 align:middle line:84%
you send your paper
in to the journal,

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:28.690 align:middle line:90%
and they evaluate your paper.

00:29:28.690 --> 00:29:31.490 align:middle line:84%
And then they tell
you how good--

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:34.360 align:middle line:84%
they give you some
answer, r of q,

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:36.640 align:middle line:84%
which is like, if you
can make your paper--

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:39.610 align:middle line:84%
get your craftsmanship
up to quality r of q,

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:40.860 align:middle line:90%
we would take your paper.

00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:42.830 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, no, it's
not good enough.

00:29:42.830 --> 00:29:44.650 align:middle line:90%
So here's your paper.

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:50.200 align:middle line:84%
In general, actually, you send a
paper to a lot of top economics

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:53.440 align:middle line:84%
journals today, you're going to
get five referee reports that

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:55.780 align:middle line:84%
are going to be a total
of like 18 pages long

00:29:55.780 --> 00:30:00.200 align:middle line:84%
and have 67 comments of things
you could improve in your paper.

00:30:00.200 --> 00:30:04.210 align:middle line:84%
And you then decide--
they're telling you

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:07.040 align:middle line:84%
how much you have to do to get
the paper into the journal.

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:10.330 align:middle line:84%
And then authors decide how
much time of their remaining

00:30:10.330 --> 00:30:14.590 align:middle line:84%
time between 0 and 1 minus tq
to spend improving the paper.

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:18.010 align:middle line:84%
And time spent improving the
craftsmanship increases r

00:30:18.010 --> 00:30:23.420 align:middle line:84%
to h of tr plus eta, where eta
is again some random variable.

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:26.120 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes you're able
to do the things well,

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:28.200 align:middle line:84%
sometimes you're not able
to do the things well.

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:33.560 align:middle line:84%
And then I make eta, again,
a uniform distribution.

00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:36.530 align:middle line:84%
And then what
editors do is editors

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:40.430 align:middle line:84%
accept the highest quality
papers that are resubmitted.

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:42.680 align:middle line:84%
The editors all
also know alpha t.

00:30:42.680 --> 00:30:46.560 align:middle line:84%
They all know the current
market weights on these things.

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:48.740 align:middle line:84%
The editors, though,
don't use zt.

00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:50.900 align:middle line:84%
The editors, they
know how many papers

00:30:50.900 --> 00:30:53.570 align:middle line:84%
they're supposed to accept,
so they just accept the best.

00:30:53.570 --> 00:30:55.190 align:middle line:84%
Even if the referee
has told people

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:56.925 align:middle line:84%
to do more than was
feasible, the editors

00:30:56.925 --> 00:30:58.550 align:middle line:84%
have to go against
the referee and say,

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:00.030 align:middle line:90%
I have to accept something.

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:03.260 align:middle line:84%
And so the editors just
accept some fraction tau

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:05.690 align:middle line:84%
of papers that are highest
on this metric alpha q

00:31:05.690 --> 00:31:06.835 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 minus alpha r.

00:31:06.835 --> 00:31:09.370 align:middle line:90%


00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:13.630 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, that's my model of
academics writing and revising

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:14.130 align:middle line:90%
papers.

00:31:14.130 --> 00:31:17.100 align:middle line:90%


00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:19.710 align:middle line:90%
First observation.

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:24.383 align:middle line:84%
This model has a continuum
of consistent social norms.

00:31:24.383 --> 00:31:27.420 align:middle line:90%


00:31:27.420 --> 00:31:29.580 align:middle line:84%
If you think about it,
if I pick any alpha,

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:33.030 align:middle line:84%
if I pick alpha equals
1 so only q counts,

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:35.010 align:middle line:84%
if only q counts, what
people are going to do

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:37.410 align:middle line:84%
is just spend 100% of
their time producing ideas,

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:39.780 align:middle line:84%
have zero craftsmanship,
and there's

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:43.810 align:middle line:84%
going to be some level z
that clears the market.

00:31:43.810 --> 00:31:48.780 align:middle line:84%
So if you spend 100% of
your time on q quality,

00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:51.510 align:middle line:84%
a fraction tau of people
are going to get qualities

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:54.210 align:middle line:84%
above this level,
and 1 minus tau

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:57.270 align:middle line:84%
are going to get qualities
below that level.

00:31:57.270 --> 00:31:58.685 align:middle line:90%
And that clears the market.

00:31:58.685 --> 00:32:01.470 align:middle line:90%


00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:04.650 align:middle line:84%
If you choose alpha
equals 0, then people

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:06.720 align:middle line:84%
are going to spend zero
time thinking up ideas,

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:10.530 align:middle line:84%
and they're just going
to have very, very

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:14.490 align:middle line:84%
complicated estimations or
proofs of their non-ideas.

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:16.980 align:middle line:84%
They're going to have incredibly
high levels of r quality,

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:18.980 align:middle line:84%
but they're going to have
no ideas in the paper.

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:21.640 align:middle line:84%
And again, the people
who get the best draws--

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:23.290 align:middle line:90%
everyone spends time 1.

00:32:23.290 --> 00:32:26.350 align:middle line:84%
The people who get the best
draws here, the tau fraction,

00:32:26.350 --> 00:32:28.315 align:middle line:84%
get the best draws, get
their papers published.

00:32:28.315 --> 00:32:29.940 align:middle line:84%
The other people get
the bad draws on r

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:31.482 align:middle line:84%
and don't get their
papers published.

00:32:31.482 --> 00:32:33.910 align:middle line:84%
And then, for every
weight in between,

00:32:33.910 --> 00:32:36.820 align:middle line:84%
people are going to spend some
time in q and some time in r,

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:38.710 align:middle line:84%
and there's some
quality threshold

00:32:38.710 --> 00:32:40.160 align:middle line:90%
that clears the market.

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:43.900 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense,
there's nothing

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:47.890 align:middle line:84%
in this model that pins down
whether we care about main ideas

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:49.820 align:middle line:84%
or whether we care
about craftsmanship.

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:51.740 align:middle line:84%
Profession could have
any set of norms.

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:52.845 align:middle line:90%
They're all stable.

00:32:52.845 --> 00:32:55.980 align:middle line:90%


00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:58.920 align:middle line:84%
And this would be an example
of what this equilibrium would

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:00.275 align:middle line:90%
look like.

00:33:00.275 --> 00:33:02.650 align:middle line:84%
This is a weight where people
are putting weight on both.

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:04.698 align:middle line:90%
So people choose some time q.

00:33:04.698 --> 00:33:05.740 align:middle line:90%
It must be a pretty high.

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:08.130 align:middle line:90%
People put some weight on q.

00:33:08.130 --> 00:33:10.530 align:middle line:84%
This is equal
weights, looks like.

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:13.660 align:middle line:84%
People put some weight on
q that's between 0 and 1.

00:33:13.660 --> 00:33:17.010 align:middle line:90%
They come up with ideas.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:19.590 align:middle line:84%
If at the end of your time q
you end up with a bad idea,

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:21.382 align:middle line:84%
you send it to the
journal, and the journal

00:33:21.382 --> 00:33:24.510 align:middle line:84%
is like, in order to
get your paper accepted,

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:26.820 align:middle line:84%
you're going to have to reach
this super high threshold

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:30.070 align:middle line:84%
on craftsmanship because you
don't have much of an idea,

00:33:30.070 --> 00:33:31.980 align:middle line:84%
you look at it and
you're like, given

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:35.460 align:middle line:84%
that I made the
distribution that r

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:42.120 align:middle line:84%
is h of tr plus eta, where
eta is uniform 0 sigma,

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:43.890 align:middle line:84%
there's an upper
bound to how good

00:33:43.890 --> 00:33:47.290 align:middle line:84%
my craftsmanship can be even
if I spend all the time on it.

00:33:47.290 --> 00:33:49.218 align:middle line:90%
So you've got a bad idea.

00:33:49.218 --> 00:33:50.260 align:middle line:90%
They tell you what to do.

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:51.910 align:middle line:84%
You're like, there's
no way I can do that.

00:33:51.910 --> 00:33:52.618 align:middle line:90%
You just give up.

00:33:52.618 --> 00:33:55.780 align:middle line:84%
You don't revise your
paper, and you go home.

00:33:55.780 --> 00:34:01.120 align:middle line:84%
If your paper is better,
you revise it to death.

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:04.390 align:middle line:90%
You set tr to be 1 minus tq.

00:34:04.390 --> 00:34:06.220 align:middle line:84%
You do all the
revisions you can.

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:08.760 align:middle line:90%
You get this draw on r quality.

00:34:08.760 --> 00:34:10.800 align:middle line:84%
And if you get a good
draw, you get accepted.

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:13.409 align:middle line:84%
You get a bad draw,
you get rejected.

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:16.920 align:middle line:84%
And then, if your
quality is super high,

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:19.740 align:middle line:84%
then they recognize your quality
is super high and they tell you,

00:34:19.740 --> 00:34:21.810 align:middle line:90%
you only need to do this--

00:34:21.810 --> 00:34:24.130 align:middle line:84%
reach this level of r
quality to get accepted.

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:27.750 align:middle line:84%
So what you do is there,
you're able to get it accepted

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:29.648 align:middle line:90%
with probability 1 here.

00:34:29.648 --> 00:34:31.940 align:middle line:84%
And up here, you can get it
accepted with probability 1

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:34.560 align:middle line:84%
and even take a vacation,
some vacation time.

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:36.820 align:middle line:90%
So here, you take some time off.

00:34:36.820 --> 00:34:39.659 align:middle line:84%
But any of these papers that
are super high quality, they get

00:34:39.659 --> 00:34:42.960 align:middle line:84%
accepted with probability
1, and you just do the--

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:50.155 align:middle line:84%
you set-- you choose h of tr
star is just equal to the thing

00:34:50.155 --> 00:34:52.780 align:middle line:84%
that you're being asked to do so
that even if you get the worst

00:34:52.780 --> 00:34:55.389 align:middle line:84%
data draw possible, the paper
gets accepted with probability

00:34:55.389 --> 00:34:56.139 align:middle line:90%
1.

00:34:56.139 --> 00:34:59.710 align:middle line:84%
So you ensure that
your paper gets in.

00:34:59.710 --> 00:35:03.400 align:middle line:84%
In my model, people have these
lexicographic preferences

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:06.500 align:middle line:84%
over paper acceptance
and leisure.

00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:08.042 align:middle line:84%
So anything that
adds any probability

00:35:08.042 --> 00:35:09.792 align:middle line:84%
to your paper getting
accepted, you do it,

00:35:09.792 --> 00:35:12.460 align:middle line:84%
and you only take leisure when
your probability of getting in

00:35:12.460 --> 00:35:16.930 align:middle line:84%
is 0 or when it's
0 marginal impact.

00:35:16.930 --> 00:35:17.540 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:22.420 align:middle line:84%
So this is what we
think would happen.

00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:26.170 align:middle line:84%
And so that's what an
equilibrium would look like.

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:28.750 align:middle line:84%
And then the
question about, where

00:35:28.750 --> 00:35:31.450 align:middle line:84%
do these dynamics come
from, because it's

00:35:31.450 --> 00:35:33.980 align:middle line:84%
gotten harder and harder to
publish papers over the years.

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:36.310 align:middle line:90%
Why do I think that happens?

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:39.350 align:middle line:84%
What I then add is an
overconfidence bias.

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:41.980 align:middle line:84%
So everybody thinks
their papers are

00:35:41.980 --> 00:35:43.630 align:middle line:90%
better than they actually are.

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:45.638 align:middle line:84%
So either you think
your q is better

00:35:45.638 --> 00:35:47.680 align:middle line:84%
than it really is, your
idea is more interesting,

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:49.690 align:middle line:84%
or you think your
craftsmanship is better

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:52.338 align:middle line:84%
than your craftsmanship really
is because you're using the best

00:35:52.338 --> 00:35:54.880 align:middle line:84%
techniques you know about and
you think, therefore, I'm state

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:56.810 align:middle line:90%
of the art, when you're not.

00:35:56.810 --> 00:35:59.410 align:middle line:84%
But everybody just
thinks their paper

00:35:59.410 --> 00:36:04.570 align:middle line:84%
is epsilon better than other
people think their paper is.

00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:06.160 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:11.920 align:middle line:84%
And so what's going to happen
when people are learning is,

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:16.080 align:middle line:84%
this norm with an
overconfidence bias

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:19.435 align:middle line:84%
is going to confuse people
because they're going to be--

00:36:19.435 --> 00:36:21.310 align:middle line:84%
two things are going to
be happening to them.

00:36:21.310 --> 00:36:23.160 align:middle line:84%
One is, they're going
to be submitting papers,

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:24.827 align:middle line:84%
and they're going to
be told that you've

00:36:24.827 --> 00:36:27.690 align:middle line:84%
got to reach this really,
really high-- well, what

00:36:27.690 --> 00:36:30.290 align:middle line:84%
seems to you to be a really,
really high line because you

00:36:30.290 --> 00:36:32.790 align:middle line:84%
think your paper is better than
it is, and you're like, wow,

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:34.620 align:middle line:84%
it's really, really
hard to get a paper in.

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:36.570 align:middle line:84%
For my paper to get
accepted, I have

00:36:36.570 --> 00:36:38.610 align:middle line:84%
to do this laundry
list of 67 things

00:36:38.610 --> 00:36:40.410 align:middle line:90%
better than I'm doing today.

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:44.370 align:middle line:84%
And yet you also referee
papers and you say,

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:45.850 align:middle line:90%
this is not as good as mine.

00:36:45.850 --> 00:36:47.080 align:middle line:90%
You should reject it.

00:36:47.080 --> 00:36:49.000 align:middle line:84%
And then you see the
journal taking the paper.

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:51.250 align:middle line:84%
And so you have this sort
of cognitive dissonance.

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:53.680 align:middle line:84%
It's like, why is it that
I can't get my papers in

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:56.010 align:middle line:84%
and these mediocre things
either that I refereed

00:36:56.010 --> 00:36:58.950 align:middle line:84%
or I see in the journal
are getting published?

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:01.380 align:middle line:84%
And so you have to try
to reconcile those two

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:03.240 align:middle line:84%
things in your mind
and think, maybe I

00:37:03.240 --> 00:37:05.245 align:middle line:84%
misunderstood the
alpha z process.

00:37:05.245 --> 00:37:08.790 align:middle line:90%


00:37:08.790 --> 00:37:13.260 align:middle line:84%
Again, here, I could do
something rational where--

00:37:13.260 --> 00:37:15.480 align:middle line:84%
something fully
rational where people

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:19.170 align:middle line:84%
have this prior
overall alphas and z's

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:23.310 align:middle line:84%
that the world might have and
a prior over the whole process

00:37:23.310 --> 00:37:28.390 align:middle line:84%
that is being used to generate
acceptance and rejections.

00:37:28.390 --> 00:37:30.600 align:middle line:84%
And think about the
rational Bayesian updating.

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:33.780 align:middle line:84%
When you're getting these two
conflicting pieces of evidence,

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:34.980 align:middle line:90%
how do you best--

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:37.380 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, it's
a Bayesian updating

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:39.480 align:middle line:84%
where the true
state of the world

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:44.790 align:middle line:84%
is outside the
support of your prior

00:37:44.790 --> 00:37:46.950 align:middle line:84%
because you don't think
that you're biased.

00:37:46.950 --> 00:37:51.060 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, what I did
instead was think about just

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:52.510 align:middle line:90%
a different objective function.

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:57.040 align:middle line:84%
That is, I'm basically
getting two types of evidence.

00:37:57.040 --> 00:38:04.020 align:middle line:84%
One is, I'm being asked, these
are the r of q's on my paper.

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:08.365 align:middle line:84%
And I'm trying to figure
out, what is the correct--

00:38:08.365 --> 00:38:10.490 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to figure out,
what is the correct line?

00:38:10.490 --> 00:38:12.130 align:middle line:84%
And this is what
I think r of q is

00:38:12.130 --> 00:38:14.980 align:middle line:90%
on the observations on my paper.

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:17.770 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to say that people
are in some-- one thing they're

00:38:17.770 --> 00:38:21.040 align:middle line:84%
trying to do is fit
a line to the data

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:22.720 align:middle line:84%
that they're getting
on their own papers

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:25.307 align:middle line:84%
with some least
squares procedure.

00:38:25.307 --> 00:38:26.890 align:middle line:84%
But then the other
thing they're doing

00:38:26.890 --> 00:38:30.950 align:middle line:84%
is they're seeing other papers
are accepted or rejected.

00:38:30.950 --> 00:38:33.580 align:middle line:84%
And every time a
paper-- if this paper

00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:37.630 align:middle line:84%
is accepted but this
paper is also accepted,

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:40.390 align:middle line:84%
and this one is rejected
and this one is rejected

00:38:40.390 --> 00:38:44.657 align:middle line:84%
and this one is rejected,
and this one's accepted,

00:38:44.657 --> 00:38:46.240 align:middle line:84%
they're trying to,
in some sense, also

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:49.570 align:middle line:84%
fit a line to this data that
separates the accepted papers

00:38:49.570 --> 00:38:52.060 align:middle line:90%
from the rejected papers.

00:38:52.060 --> 00:38:54.730 align:middle line:84%
And what I do, entirely to
make the model tractable,

00:38:54.730 --> 00:38:57.430 align:middle line:84%
is say that you're using
a least squares loss

00:38:57.430 --> 00:39:00.490 align:middle line:84%
function for the things you're
told about your own papers,

00:39:00.490 --> 00:39:03.040 align:middle line:84%
and you're using a sum of
absolute deviations loss

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:05.930 align:middle line:84%
function for the
acceptance and rejections

00:39:05.930 --> 00:39:08.703 align:middle line:84%
and trying to fit the two data
together with the same line.

00:39:08.703 --> 00:39:11.120 align:middle line:84%
And what's going on is that
they're-- actually, basically,

00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:12.200 align:middle line:90%
there are--

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:14.550 align:middle line:84%
what's happening is in reality
is there are two lines.

00:39:14.550 --> 00:39:18.170 align:middle line:84%
This is the r of q
line, what you perceive

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:19.970 align:middle line:84%
you're being asked
to do, and this

00:39:19.970 --> 00:39:22.950 align:middle line:84%
is the acceptance-rejection
line, which is lower.

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:24.620 align:middle line:84%
And you think you're
being asked to do

00:39:24.620 --> 00:39:26.995 align:middle line:84%
more because you think your
quality is better than it is.

00:39:26.995 --> 00:39:29.863 align:middle line:84%
So the data is really coming
in on these two lines,

00:39:29.863 --> 00:39:32.030 align:middle line:84%
and you're trying to fit a
single line to this data,

00:39:32.030 --> 00:39:34.460 align:middle line:84%
not recognizing that the
observations from your papers

00:39:34.460 --> 00:39:35.918 align:middle line:84%
are different from
the observations

00:39:35.918 --> 00:39:37.160 align:middle line:90%
on the other papers.

00:39:37.160 --> 00:39:39.800 align:middle line:84%
And the observation
of the paper is

00:39:39.800 --> 00:39:43.650 align:middle line:84%
if people have views
on this, what happens?

00:39:43.650 --> 00:39:50.010 align:middle line:84%
What happens is, people perceive
this line to be the line.

00:39:50.010 --> 00:39:52.810 align:middle line:84%
And everyone thinks it's
harder to publish than it is.

00:39:52.810 --> 00:39:55.900 align:middle line:84%
They always tell the authors
when they're refereeing,

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:57.990 align:middle line:84%
you need to get really,
really good to get in,

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:01.910 align:middle line:84%
and you need to reach
this high r of q line.

00:40:01.910 --> 00:40:04.370 align:middle line:84%
But then the editors
can't fill the journal

00:40:04.370 --> 00:40:06.990 align:middle line:84%
with papers who reach
the high r of q line.

00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:08.510 align:middle line:84%
So there are these
people down here

00:40:08.510 --> 00:40:12.320 align:middle line:84%
who had the medium quality
ideas that revised them

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:15.320 align:middle line:84%
to death, who fell just short
of what the referees told

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:15.850 align:middle line:90%
them to do.

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:17.600 align:middle line:84%
And then the editors
take the paper anyway

00:40:17.600 --> 00:40:19.980 align:middle line:84%
because they need
to fill the journal.

00:40:19.980 --> 00:40:28.120 align:middle line:84%
And so they take the papers
that were slightly lower quality

00:40:28.120 --> 00:40:30.360 align:middle line:84%
on the r dimension than
what they were told to do.

00:40:30.360 --> 00:40:33.040 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the data
actually looks like.

00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:36.340 align:middle line:84%
And then when people are
trying to fit these two data

00:40:36.340 --> 00:40:39.220 align:middle line:84%
sources together,
the r of q data

00:40:39.220 --> 00:40:42.130 align:middle line:84%
and the acceptance-rejection
data, the best way

00:40:42.130 --> 00:40:47.800 align:middle line:84%
to do that is by tilting the
line down because the surprise

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:52.810 align:middle line:84%
acceptances are papers that
are low q, high r, and get in.

00:40:52.810 --> 00:40:54.670 align:middle line:84%
And so the best way
that you can explain

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:56.470 align:middle line:84%
that on these
really great papers,

00:40:56.470 --> 00:40:59.650 align:middle line:84%
I'm being asked to do a
lot, and yet down here I

00:40:59.650 --> 00:41:03.550 align:middle line:84%
see these low q, high r
papers getting in, is to say,

00:41:03.550 --> 00:41:07.900 align:middle line:84%
oh, I think it must be that
the best way to fit this data

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:11.500 align:middle line:84%
is to tilt the line down
and think that r quality is

00:41:11.500 --> 00:41:13.250 align:middle line:90%
more important than I thought.

00:41:13.250 --> 00:41:16.570 align:middle line:84%
And these high r
papers are getting in

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:18.970 align:middle line:90%
because r quality matters more.

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:21.490 align:middle line:84%
And so the argument is that
what's going to happen is,

00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:24.430 align:middle line:84%
people are just going to keep
being surprised that papers get

00:41:24.430 --> 00:41:26.390 align:middle line:90%
accepted that they refereed.

00:41:26.390 --> 00:41:29.330 align:middle line:84%
The surprise acceptances
are overwhelmingly low q,

00:41:29.330 --> 00:41:30.155 align:middle line:90%
high r papers.

00:41:30.155 --> 00:41:32.870 align:middle line:90%


00:41:32.870 --> 00:41:35.960 align:middle line:84%
And so the best way to explain
that those surprise acceptances

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:39.560 align:middle line:84%
and yet the high demands being
made for the high q papers

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:42.200 align:middle line:84%
are tilting the line
and thinking r quality

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:44.070 align:middle line:84%
is a little bit more
important than I am.

00:41:44.070 --> 00:41:45.570 align:middle line:84%
And so we end up
with this situation

00:41:45.570 --> 00:41:49.760 align:middle line:84%
where just perpetually, everyone
always is holding people

00:41:49.760 --> 00:41:53.617 align:middle line:84%
to standards that are slightly
too high, and then slightly

00:41:53.617 --> 00:41:55.700 align:middle line:84%
twisting their views to
think about the importance

00:41:55.700 --> 00:41:58.610 align:middle line:84%
of craftsmanship versus
the importance of ideas.

00:41:58.610 --> 00:42:03.540 align:middle line:84%
And this graph
shows you-- if you

00:42:03.540 --> 00:42:06.570 align:middle line:84%
think of this as the
set of social norms

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:08.910 align:middle line:84%
that would all be consistent
with alpha on this axis

00:42:08.910 --> 00:42:11.880 align:middle line:84%
and z on this axis, we're
always staying slightly

00:42:11.880 --> 00:42:16.300 align:middle line:90%
above that set of social norms.

00:42:16.300 --> 00:42:18.000 align:middle line:84%
And then this
dynamics of this model

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:19.860 align:middle line:84%
would just slowly drift
and drift and drift

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:20.980 align:middle line:90%
and drift and drift.

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:23.400 align:middle line:90%
And the argument is that--

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:26.610 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to argue that this
was a 30-year disequilibrium

00:42:26.610 --> 00:42:27.220 align:middle line:90%
process.

00:42:27.220 --> 00:42:29.290 align:middle line:84%
For 30 years, we've
been out of equilibrium.

00:42:29.290 --> 00:42:32.160 align:middle line:84%
Referees are always
slightly too high.

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:34.410 align:middle line:84%
I'm just trying to
reconcile the difference

00:42:34.410 --> 00:42:37.230 align:middle line:84%
in their mind between the
slightly too high standards they

00:42:37.230 --> 00:42:39.777 align:middle line:84%
think are being applied with
the acceptances they see.

00:42:39.777 --> 00:42:42.360 align:middle line:84%
They just keep thinking that it
must be the craftsmanship that

00:42:42.360 --> 00:42:43.173 align:middle line:90%
got that one in.

00:42:43.173 --> 00:42:45.090 align:middle line:84%
And then they keep just
changing their beliefs

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:46.900 align:middle line:84%
about the importance
of craftsmanship.

00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:51.420 align:middle line:90%


00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:53.240 align:middle line:90%
Any questions there?

00:42:53.240 --> 00:42:55.250 align:middle line:84%
So again, the
rule-of-thumb approach

00:42:55.250 --> 00:42:58.970 align:middle line:84%
here is just I'm trying to
say what the referees do.

00:42:58.970 --> 00:43:03.320 align:middle line:84%
And I've actually-- some chance
I will rewrite this paper doing

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:04.820 align:middle line:84%
the fully Bayesian
version, which

00:43:04.820 --> 00:43:07.520 align:middle line:84%
I think there's been big
advances in Bayesian learning

00:43:07.520 --> 00:43:11.120 align:middle line:84%
with misspecified priors
in the last 20 years,

00:43:11.120 --> 00:43:13.460 align:middle line:84%
where I think maybe now I
could solve this Bayesian

00:43:13.460 --> 00:43:16.220 align:middle line:84%
model with misspecified priors
and show that kind of what

00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:19.100 align:middle line:84%
I say happens here, happens
in a fully Bayesian model

00:43:19.100 --> 00:43:20.258 align:middle line:90%
with misspecified priors.

00:43:20.258 --> 00:43:22.550 align:middle line:84%
But I think that the idea of
the rule-of-thumb approach

00:43:22.550 --> 00:43:26.132 align:middle line:84%
is just to say, think about
what you think people would do.

00:43:26.132 --> 00:43:28.590 align:middle line:84%
Think about what you think they
would do that's reasonable.

00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:29.882 align:middle line:90%
I don't think it's reasonable--

00:43:29.882 --> 00:43:32.372 align:middle line:84%
I think it seems like
referees are fairly naive,

00:43:32.372 --> 00:43:34.830 align:middle line:84%
and this is how they're trying
to reconcile the two things.

00:43:34.830 --> 00:43:35.580 align:middle line:90%
This could happen.

00:43:35.580 --> 00:43:38.420 align:middle line:84%
And then write down a model
that you can actually solve.

00:43:38.420 --> 00:43:41.205 align:middle line:84%
And like, why do I
have a quadratic loss

00:43:41.205 --> 00:43:43.580 align:middle line:84%
function in one part and a
least absolute deviations loss

00:43:43.580 --> 00:43:44.455 align:middle line:90%
function in one part?

00:43:44.455 --> 00:43:47.370 align:middle line:84%
And it's exactly because that
was the model I could solve.

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:48.860 align:middle line:84%
We might as well
solve-- write down

00:43:48.860 --> 00:43:54.100 align:middle line:84%
models we can solve and
say what we can about them.

00:43:54.100 --> 00:43:56.680 align:middle line:84%
The, let me say,
empirics on this,

00:43:56.680 --> 00:44:00.820 align:middle line:84%
I have another kind of
depressing paper in the JPE,

00:44:00.820 --> 00:44:03.203 align:middle line:84%
looking at how long
it takes a paper

00:44:03.203 --> 00:44:04.870 align:middle line:84%
to get accepted from
the time it's first

00:44:04.870 --> 00:44:07.450 align:middle line:90%
submitted historically.

00:44:07.450 --> 00:44:13.300 align:middle line:84%
And in the 1970s, papers were
typically getting accepted

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:16.270 align:middle line:84%
at all the top journals 6 to
8 months after they were first

00:44:16.270 --> 00:44:17.550 align:middle line:90%
accepted.

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:20.200 align:middle line:84%
And it's kind of remarkable
when you realize, at the time,

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:22.780 align:middle line:84%
the Xerox machine had
not been invented.

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:25.640 align:middle line:84%
And so how did
refereeing a paper work?

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:30.850 align:middle line:84%
You would type up two or
three copies of your paper.

00:44:30.850 --> 00:44:32.758 align:middle line:84%
You would send-- or
actually, in the 1960s,

00:44:32.758 --> 00:44:35.050 align:middle line:84%
you would send in a single
draft of the manuscript that

00:44:35.050 --> 00:44:36.340 align:middle line:90%
your secretary typed.

00:44:36.340 --> 00:44:38.470 align:middle line:84%
But you would send
the paper in--

00:44:38.470 --> 00:44:41.890 align:middle line:84%
so '60s, certainly, your
secretary typed one copy

00:44:41.890 --> 00:44:43.810 align:middle line:84%
of the paper, you sent
it to the journal,

00:44:43.810 --> 00:44:45.620 align:middle line:90%
they sent it to the referee.

00:44:45.620 --> 00:44:48.513 align:middle line:84%
The referee read the
paper, made comments,

00:44:48.513 --> 00:44:49.930 align:middle line:84%
sent it back to
the journal office

00:44:49.930 --> 00:44:51.800 align:middle line:90%
because that was the only copy.

00:44:51.800 --> 00:44:54.590 align:middle line:84%
The journal office then sent
the paper to a second referee.

00:44:54.590 --> 00:44:56.900 align:middle line:84%
That second referee made
comments, sent it back

00:44:56.900 --> 00:44:58.110 align:middle line:90%
to the journal office.

00:44:58.110 --> 00:45:02.575 align:middle line:84%
The journal office made comments
and mailed you a decision.

00:45:02.575 --> 00:45:03.950 align:middle line:84%
Somehow, at the
time, papers were

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:07.850 align:middle line:84%
getting published 5, 6 months
after they were submitted.

00:45:07.850 --> 00:45:10.910 align:middle line:84%
Starting in the '70s, when
the revise and resubmit was

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:14.180 align:middle line:84%
invented, things just got
longer and longer and longer

00:45:14.180 --> 00:45:14.910 align:middle line:90%
and longer.

00:45:14.910 --> 00:45:18.772 align:middle line:84%
And by the year 2000, some
journals were averaging 2

00:45:18.772 --> 00:45:21.230 align:middle line:84%
and 1/2 years from the time a
paper was submitted to get it

00:45:21.230 --> 00:45:24.990 align:middle line:84%
published, some were averaging
two years, and so on.

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:30.253 align:middle line:84%
And this is the time that's
conditional on a good outcome.

00:45:30.253 --> 00:45:31.670 align:middle line:84%
The way the process
tends to work,

00:45:31.670 --> 00:45:36.120 align:middle line:84%
though, is the top journals
have a 96% rejection rate.

00:45:36.120 --> 00:45:38.902 align:middle line:84%
So you send it to journal
one, they screw around with it

00:45:38.902 --> 00:45:40.860 align:middle line:84%
for a year and a half,
and then they reject it.

00:45:40.860 --> 00:45:42.110 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
one, and then they

00:45:42.110 --> 00:45:44.100 align:middle line:84%
mess around with it for six
months, and they reject it.

00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:46.170 align:middle line:84%
And the third one, it's a
year, and then they reject it.

00:45:46.170 --> 00:45:47.545 align:middle line:84%
And then finally,
you get someone

00:45:47.545 --> 00:45:50.360 align:middle line:84%
to spend three years revising
it before they then--

00:45:50.360 --> 00:45:52.310 align:middle line:84%
two or three years, and
then they publish it.

00:45:52.310 --> 00:45:55.670 align:middle line:84%
So actually, I have this
very depressing statistic

00:45:55.670 --> 00:45:59.450 align:middle line:84%
that if you look at the
absolute stars on the IO market

00:45:59.450 --> 00:46:03.380 align:middle line:84%
who do empirical IO and how long
it takes them to get their job

00:46:03.380 --> 00:46:06.650 align:middle line:84%
market papers published,
the median time is now

00:46:06.650 --> 00:46:10.430 align:middle line:84%
7 years, which, you
know, it's a long time,

00:46:10.430 --> 00:46:12.920 align:middle line:84%
because then you spent also
three years writing that paper

00:46:12.920 --> 00:46:15.540 align:middle line:84%
before you first
graduated from school.

00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:16.760 align:middle line:90%
So.

00:46:16.760 --> 00:46:19.915 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, I hope
future generations

00:46:19.915 --> 00:46:22.290 align:middle line:84%
will recognize what they're
doing and solve this problem.

00:46:22.290 --> 00:46:26.750 align:middle line:84%
And I've made some attempts
with limited success.

00:46:26.750 --> 00:46:29.210 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:46:29.210 --> 00:46:29.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:29.770 --> 00:46:31.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How important
is it in the model

00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:33.380 align:middle line:84%
that the authors
perfectly observe

00:46:33.380 --> 00:46:36.320 align:middle line:90%
the quality of their ideas?

00:46:36.320 --> 00:46:38.798 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: So I don't
think it's at all important.

00:46:38.798 --> 00:46:40.340 align:middle line:84%
I think what's going
on is I'm having

00:46:40.340 --> 00:46:49.200 align:middle line:84%
the authors do this sort of
least squares fit of this.

00:46:49.200 --> 00:46:51.260 align:middle line:84%
So they're being asked
to do-- every time

00:46:51.260 --> 00:46:54.590 align:middle line:84%
you submit a paper with quality
q, you're being asked to r of q.

00:46:54.590 --> 00:46:58.890 align:middle line:84%
And you just fit a least
squares line to that.

00:46:58.890 --> 00:47:01.166 align:middle line:90%
So if there was sort of--

00:47:01.166 --> 00:47:06.230 align:middle line:84%
if your paper qualities were
like this, fitting a line to it,

00:47:06.230 --> 00:47:08.720 align:middle line:84%
I think, would do
roughly the same thing.

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:12.740 align:middle line:84%
What's important is the argument
that the very high quality

00:47:12.740 --> 00:47:14.450 align:middle line:84%
papers, you're
asked to do a lot,

00:47:14.450 --> 00:47:16.520 align:middle line:84%
and they do all get
accepted because you

00:47:16.520 --> 00:47:19.010 align:middle line:90%
do what you're being asked.

00:47:19.010 --> 00:47:21.170 align:middle line:84%
And the low quality
ones are the ones

00:47:21.170 --> 00:47:24.480 align:middle line:84%
that get in not doing
what they were asked.

00:47:24.480 --> 00:47:26.852 align:middle line:84%
So the high quality
ones don't get

00:47:26.852 --> 00:47:29.060 align:middle line:84%
in not doing what they were
asked because they always

00:47:29.060 --> 00:47:30.780 align:middle line:90%
do what they were asked enough.

00:47:30.780 --> 00:47:33.050 align:middle line:90%
That's, I think, the main thing.

00:47:33.050 --> 00:47:35.750 align:middle line:84%
But the randomness I
don't think would affect--

00:47:35.750 --> 00:47:38.300 align:middle line:84%
on either one
shouldn't affect it.

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:41.150 align:middle line:84%
Let me say, advertising
is not something

00:47:41.150 --> 00:47:44.810 align:middle line:84%
I can do any-- do
justice in this paper.

00:47:44.810 --> 00:47:46.448 align:middle line:84%
There is a field
called marketing.

00:47:46.448 --> 00:47:48.740 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if any of you
are from the Sloan Marketing

00:47:48.740 --> 00:47:51.140 align:middle line:84%
Department, but most
business schools

00:47:51.140 --> 00:47:52.500 align:middle line:90%
have marketing departments.

00:47:52.500 --> 00:47:55.153 align:middle line:84%
Marketing departments have
lots and lots of professors.

00:47:55.153 --> 00:47:57.070 align:middle line:84%
There are like a thousand
marketing professors

00:47:57.070 --> 00:47:58.130 align:middle line:90%
in the United States.

00:47:58.130 --> 00:47:59.830 align:middle line:84%
There are many, many
marketing journals,

00:47:59.830 --> 00:48:01.610 align:middle line:84%
just like there are
economics journals.

00:48:01.610 --> 00:48:04.370 align:middle line:84%
Those marketing journals
are full of papers.

00:48:04.370 --> 00:48:07.840 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, people in
marketing do advertising.

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:09.910 align:middle line:84%
They teach entire--
all of their courses

00:48:09.910 --> 00:48:12.810 align:middle line:84%
are entirely marketing, not one
week of my class or something

00:48:12.810 --> 00:48:13.310 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:48:13.310 --> 00:48:16.930 align:middle line:84%
So there's an enormous
literature out there.

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:19.480 align:middle line:90%
Marketing is a field where--

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:22.670 align:middle line:84%
I put here influences
on marketing.

00:48:22.670 --> 00:48:25.000 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at what's
being cited by marketing

00:48:25.000 --> 00:48:28.305 align:middle line:90%
papers, the largest--

00:48:28.305 --> 00:48:30.680 align:middle line:84%
well, obviously, mostly they
cite other marketing papers.

00:48:30.680 --> 00:48:33.970 align:middle line:84%
So this is, what non-business
things are they citing?

00:48:33.970 --> 00:48:36.040 align:middle line:84%
They do cite a lot
of psychology papers,

00:48:36.040 --> 00:48:39.370 align:middle line:84%
and there's been of a rise of
psychology in marketing over

00:48:39.370 --> 00:48:43.210 align:middle line:84%
the last 40-year time span,
where people in marketing are

00:48:43.210 --> 00:48:46.330 align:middle line:84%
paying more and more attention
to what psychologists are saying

00:48:46.330 --> 00:48:48.200 align:middle line:84%
and citing what
psychologists are saying.

00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:50.350 align:middle line:84%
There's also been
an increasing trend

00:48:50.350 --> 00:48:52.880 align:middle line:84%
in citing what
economists are doing.

00:48:52.880 --> 00:48:57.040 align:middle line:84%
And so now it's 6% of the
citations in marketing papers

00:48:57.040 --> 00:48:59.780 align:middle line:84%
are to economics papers, rather
than to marketing papers.

00:48:59.780 --> 00:49:02.540 align:middle line:84%
So there's a
substantial influence

00:49:02.540 --> 00:49:04.170 align:middle line:90%
of economics on marketing.

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:06.980 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at what within
economics are marketing people

00:49:06.980 --> 00:49:10.400 align:middle line:84%
citing, IO, which is
the yellow line here,

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:12.630 align:middle line:84%
is a big part of what's
going on in marketing.

00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:13.522 align:middle line:90%
And so there's just--

00:49:13.522 --> 00:49:15.980 align:middle line:84%
there are marketing papers that
look like psychology papers

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:17.563 align:middle line:84%
or look very different
from economics.

00:49:17.563 --> 00:49:19.340 align:middle line:84%
But there are a lot
of marketing papers

00:49:19.340 --> 00:49:21.980 align:middle line:84%
that look like IO theory
papers and that look

00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:23.550 align:middle line:90%
like empirical IO papers.

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:27.050 align:middle line:84%
So they're just-- marketing
people write all about the way

00:49:27.050 --> 00:49:31.130 align:middle line:84%
that firms can price or make
their products appealing

00:49:31.130 --> 00:49:32.220 align:middle line:90%
to customers.

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:35.720 align:middle line:84%
And they write a lot about
empirical demand estimation

00:49:35.720 --> 00:49:38.820 align:middle line:84%
and how advertising campaigns
affect demand and so on.

00:49:38.820 --> 00:49:42.025 align:middle line:84%
And both of those parts
look a lot like IO.

00:49:42.025 --> 00:49:43.400 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going
to do today is,

00:49:43.400 --> 00:49:45.980 align:middle line:84%
today, I'm just going to
do a few classic models

00:49:45.980 --> 00:49:47.450 align:middle line:90%
of advertising.

00:49:47.450 --> 00:49:49.800 align:middle line:84%
And then it's, again,
a week from Monday,

00:49:49.800 --> 00:49:54.160 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to do some
empirics on advertising.

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:56.410 align:middle line:90%
So models of advertising.

00:49:56.410 --> 00:49:58.210 align:middle line:84%
Generally, I think
you can see the way

00:49:58.210 --> 00:50:03.880 align:middle line:84%
people in the economics
literature break down three

00:50:03.880 --> 00:50:04.780 align:middle line:90%
different--

00:50:04.780 --> 00:50:06.625 align:middle line:84%
obviously, to
explain advertising,

00:50:06.625 --> 00:50:08.500 align:middle line:84%
you've got to say, why
is it that advertising

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:10.070 align:middle line:90%
changes what people purchase?

00:50:10.070 --> 00:50:12.370 align:middle line:84%
If you start with our
basic model of consumers

00:50:12.370 --> 00:50:15.160 align:middle line:90%
have preferences ui jt.

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:18.250 align:middle line:84%
If they buy product
j at time t and it's

00:50:18.250 --> 00:50:22.450 align:middle line:84%
xjt beta minus alpha pjt
and they buy the best thing,

00:50:22.450 --> 00:50:26.195 align:middle line:84%
then what does an advertisement
do if it doesn't change your--

00:50:26.195 --> 00:50:27.820 align:middle line:84%
if that's your utility
function, you're

00:50:27.820 --> 00:50:29.158 align:middle line:90%
always maximizing your utility.

00:50:29.158 --> 00:50:30.700 align:middle line:84%
Advertising shouldn't
affect anything

00:50:30.700 --> 00:50:32.260 align:middle line:90%
that goes on in the world.

00:50:32.260 --> 00:50:34.510 align:middle line:84%
And yet we see firms
spend lots and lots

00:50:34.510 --> 00:50:35.690 align:middle line:90%
of money on advertising.

00:50:35.690 --> 00:50:39.640 align:middle line:84%
It must be advertising changes
behavior or changes something

00:50:39.640 --> 00:50:40.970 align:middle line:90%
that increases profits.

00:50:40.970 --> 00:50:42.490 align:middle line:90%
What is it?

00:50:42.490 --> 00:50:46.000 align:middle line:84%
One thing, one class of
models is advertising

00:50:46.000 --> 00:50:48.010 align:middle line:90%
can have informational content.

00:50:48.010 --> 00:50:51.550 align:middle line:84%
It can tell you that
a product exists,

00:50:51.550 --> 00:50:53.200 align:middle line:84%
what the product's
price is, what

00:50:53.200 --> 00:50:55.050 align:middle line:90%
the product's attributes are.

00:50:55.050 --> 00:50:57.055 align:middle line:84%
But in some sense, it's
like a search model.

00:50:57.055 --> 00:50:59.180 align:middle line:84%
You don't know everything,
but then the advertising

00:50:59.180 --> 00:51:01.160 align:middle line:84%
tells you things that
you would otherwise

00:51:01.160 --> 00:51:05.090 align:middle line:84%
have to invest search
effort in figuring out,

00:51:05.090 --> 00:51:07.730 align:middle line:84%
and that increases the number
of people who buy the product.

00:51:07.730 --> 00:51:11.630 align:middle line:90%
And classic examples like this.

00:51:11.630 --> 00:51:12.870 align:middle line:90%
Informative advertising.

00:51:12.870 --> 00:51:16.680 align:middle line:84%
You just get an ad in the mail
or in your newspaper for Target.

00:51:16.680 --> 00:51:18.650 align:middle line:84%
And the advertisement
for Target tells you

00:51:18.650 --> 00:51:23.300 align:middle line:84%
you can get these boots for $10
and those jackets for $15 or $20

00:51:23.300 --> 00:51:25.430 align:middle line:90%
and these clothes for $4 or $5.

00:51:25.430 --> 00:51:26.930 align:middle line:84%
They show you the
pictures of things

00:51:26.930 --> 00:51:29.760 align:middle line:84%
to tell you what Target is
selling and what it costs.

00:51:29.760 --> 00:51:33.680 align:middle line:84%
And this would be
informative advertising.

00:51:33.680 --> 00:51:35.990 align:middle line:84%
A second thing that
advertising can do

00:51:35.990 --> 00:51:39.410 align:middle line:84%
is not necessarily tell you
anything about the product

00:51:39.410 --> 00:51:44.360 align:middle line:84%
itself, but just signal to you
something about the quality.

00:51:44.360 --> 00:51:46.840 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, let me
look at these two ads.

00:51:46.840 --> 00:51:51.440 align:middle line:84%
You guys are probably all too
young to remember pets.com.

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:53.300 align:middle line:84%
Pets.com in the
first internet boom--

00:51:53.300 --> 00:51:55.640 align:middle line:84%
just like when there
was amazon.com,

00:51:55.640 --> 00:51:58.700 align:middle line:84%
pets.com started up
around the same time.

00:51:58.700 --> 00:52:02.780 align:middle line:84%
Pets.com, just a
miraculous rise and fall

00:52:02.780 --> 00:52:05.450 align:middle line:84%
of a company in the
space of a year or two,

00:52:05.450 --> 00:52:08.612 align:middle line:84%
where pets.com just
burned through hundreds

00:52:08.612 --> 00:52:09.570 align:middle line:90%
of millions of dollars.

00:52:09.570 --> 00:52:10.790 align:middle line:90%
They had this sock puppet.

00:52:10.790 --> 00:52:13.160 align:middle line:84%
And they put the sock
puppet on the Super Bowl

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:16.160 align:middle line:84%
and spent millions of dollars
just having the sock puppet

00:52:16.160 --> 00:52:17.930 align:middle line:90%
just appear on the Super Bowl.

00:52:17.930 --> 00:52:20.660 align:middle line:84%
The sock puppet did not
tell you anything about--

00:52:20.660 --> 00:52:21.870 align:middle line:90%
obviously, what is pets.com?

00:52:21.870 --> 00:52:23.550 align:middle line:90%
They sell stuff for pets.

00:52:23.550 --> 00:52:25.620 align:middle line:84%
You know what's
in a Petco store.

00:52:25.620 --> 00:52:28.460 align:middle line:84%
You probably know
what's on pets.com.

00:52:28.460 --> 00:52:30.170 align:middle line:84%
But the sock puppet,
in some sense,

00:52:30.170 --> 00:52:33.830 align:middle line:84%
maybe was sort of saying
that, wow, they just spent

00:52:33.830 --> 00:52:38.000 align:middle line:84%
$2.4 million to have the
sock puppet just interviewing

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:40.160 align:middle line:90%
somebody during the Super Bowl.

00:52:40.160 --> 00:52:42.660 align:middle line:84%
It must be they think
they have a good product.

00:52:42.660 --> 00:52:44.180 align:middle line:84%
And if I actually
go to pets.com,

00:52:44.180 --> 00:52:45.950 align:middle line:84%
I'll buy stuff
because otherwise,

00:52:45.950 --> 00:52:48.110 align:middle line:84%
how could they have
burned through $2 million

00:52:48.110 --> 00:52:49.920 align:middle line:90%
in 30 seconds like that?

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:52.287 align:middle line:84%
And so you get this
sort of signaling idea.

00:52:52.287 --> 00:52:53.870 align:middle line:84%
I would say the same
thing about-- you

00:52:53.870 --> 00:52:56.960 align:middle line:90%
look at the Patagonia ad here.

00:52:56.960 --> 00:52:59.210 align:middle line:90%
Again, I don't know.

00:52:59.210 --> 00:53:01.670 align:middle line:84%
I mean, maybe it's
all Photoshopped,

00:53:01.670 --> 00:53:04.940 align:middle line:84%
but it must have been expensive
to send some guy with a camera

00:53:04.940 --> 00:53:08.240 align:middle line:84%
up to the top of some mountain
wearing those Patagonia stuff

00:53:08.240 --> 00:53:09.660 align:middle line:90%
and taking the pictures.

00:53:09.660 --> 00:53:13.798 align:middle line:84%
And it must be that their
clothing-- either this person

00:53:13.798 --> 00:53:16.340 align:middle line:84%
is really not uncomfortable up
there on that mountain wearing

00:53:16.340 --> 00:53:18.410 align:middle line:84%
the Patagonia stuff and
so the clothing is good,

00:53:18.410 --> 00:53:20.240 align:middle line:84%
or they're just
spending a lot of money

00:53:20.240 --> 00:53:23.300 align:middle line:84%
on the ads because they know
that if I bought the clothing,

00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:25.380 align:middle line:84%
I would like it or
something like that.

00:53:25.380 --> 00:53:28.550 align:middle line:84%
And then I guess the third thing
we think about advertising is

00:53:28.550 --> 00:53:30.920 align:middle line:84%
advertising can be
changing your preferences

00:53:30.920 --> 00:53:33.230 align:middle line:84%
or directly affecting
purchase decisions

00:53:33.230 --> 00:53:34.530 align:middle line:90%
of non-rational things.

00:53:34.530 --> 00:53:37.740 align:middle line:84%
So if I look at this
commercial, "Let's Grab a Beer."

00:53:37.740 --> 00:53:41.510 align:middle line:84%
And those people are having
just a great time having those

00:53:41.510 --> 00:53:45.470 align:middle line:84%
beers outdoors at some
thing, and they're all

00:53:45.470 --> 00:53:48.230 align:middle line:84%
relatively attractive or
whatever, it must be I

00:53:48.230 --> 00:53:52.250 align:middle line:84%
think that somehow, if only
I was having that beer,

00:53:52.250 --> 00:53:53.930 align:middle line:84%
my life would be
like that instead

00:53:53.930 --> 00:53:55.680 align:middle line:90%
of what my life is like today.

00:53:55.680 --> 00:53:59.880 align:middle line:84%
And I would like
to be those people.

00:53:59.880 --> 00:54:05.420 align:middle line:84%
And somehow, if I just had that
beer, this is me, or something.

00:54:05.420 --> 00:54:09.290 align:middle line:84%
It changes your
preferences from being

00:54:09.290 --> 00:54:11.320 align:middle line:84%
a person who wants to
work on a problem set

00:54:11.320 --> 00:54:13.820 align:middle line:84%
to being a person who wants to
be out on the street drinking

00:54:13.820 --> 00:54:14.340 align:middle line:90%
beer.

00:54:14.340 --> 00:54:19.410 align:middle line:84%
And your tastes change, and
therefore your purchases change.

00:54:19.410 --> 00:54:19.910 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:54:19.910 --> 00:54:24.440 align:middle line:84%
So how do-- let me see--
how do I model these,

00:54:24.440 --> 00:54:27.270 align:middle line:84%
and what do we get
out of the model?

00:54:27.270 --> 00:54:31.370 align:middle line:84%
So first classic model of
informative advertising

00:54:31.370 --> 00:54:34.250 align:middle line:90%
is Butters, 1977.

00:54:34.250 --> 00:54:40.110 align:middle line:84%
Butters was thinking about N
firms selling identical goods.

00:54:40.110 --> 00:54:42.810 align:middle line:84%
There's this unit mass
of identical consumers,

00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:45.060 align:middle line:84%
and they're all going
to get utility v minus p

00:54:45.060 --> 00:54:47.970 align:middle line:84%
if they buy this
good at price p.

00:54:47.970 --> 00:54:52.350 align:middle line:84%
All the firms have some
cost c of production.

00:54:52.350 --> 00:54:58.290 align:middle line:84%
And this is a problem where
the consumers don't know either

00:54:58.290 --> 00:55:01.320 align:middle line:84%
that the product exists or
where they would buy it.

00:55:01.320 --> 00:55:05.670 align:middle line:84%
So what the firms can do is
they can spend amount of money--

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:09.030 align:middle line:84%
so if they spend A
of x on advertising,

00:55:09.030 --> 00:55:13.560 align:middle line:84%
their ads will reach a
fraction x of consumers.

00:55:13.560 --> 00:55:17.520 align:middle line:84%
So it's, I guess, good to think
about this as putting ads out

00:55:17.520 --> 00:55:20.470 align:middle line:84%
there on the radio
or print airwaves.

00:55:20.470 --> 00:55:23.310 align:middle line:84%
And just every ad
you put out there,

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:24.940 align:middle line:84%
you're going to
hit more consumers.

00:55:24.940 --> 00:55:28.500 align:middle line:84%
There's decreasing returns
because the first ads

00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:30.340 align:middle line:90%
I send out all hit somebody.

00:55:30.340 --> 00:55:35.160 align:middle line:84%
But then the later
ads that I send out

00:55:35.160 --> 00:55:37.810 align:middle line:84%
are mostly hitting people who
already heard about my product.

00:55:37.810 --> 00:55:39.840 align:middle line:84%
And so by having them
hear about my product,

00:55:39.840 --> 00:55:44.320 align:middle line:84%
I'm just wasting duplicated
messages on them.

00:55:44.320 --> 00:55:46.000 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm sending
out these messages.

00:55:46.000 --> 00:55:47.910 align:middle line:84%
And the more people
I want to reach,

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:50.425 align:middle line:84%
the higher is the
A of x, the money

00:55:50.425 --> 00:55:51.550 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to have to spend.

00:55:51.550 --> 00:55:53.653 align:middle line:84%
And it could be that even
getting to x equals 1.

00:55:53.653 --> 00:55:55.570 align:middle line:84%
This thing could almost
asymptote to infinity.

00:55:55.570 --> 00:55:59.370 align:middle line:84%
If I'm trying to reach every
person in the world, it's hard.

00:55:59.370 --> 00:56:02.280 align:middle line:84%
And so they consider a game
where firms simultaneously

00:56:02.280 --> 00:56:04.350 align:middle line:84%
choose two things, the
amount of advertising

00:56:04.350 --> 00:56:07.930 align:middle line:84%
x they're going to do,
and the price is p.

00:56:07.930 --> 00:56:11.290 align:middle line:84%
And then consumers who
buy at least one ad

00:56:11.290 --> 00:56:14.980 align:middle line:84%
buy from the cheapest firm
that they heard about.

00:56:14.980 --> 00:56:16.550 align:middle line:84%
And again, the
goods are identical.

00:56:16.550 --> 00:56:19.900 align:middle line:84%
So it's just, if v
minus pj is positive,

00:56:19.900 --> 00:56:24.270 align:middle line:84%
then I'll buy it from the
cheapest firm I found out there.

00:56:24.270 --> 00:56:27.800 align:middle line:90%


00:56:27.800 --> 00:56:33.470 align:middle line:84%
And if I don't hear about a
firm, I can't buy from them.

00:56:33.470 --> 00:56:35.030 align:middle line:84%
This could be a good
model for things

00:56:35.030 --> 00:56:36.962 align:middle line:84%
advertised through
infomercials on TV also,

00:56:36.962 --> 00:56:37.920 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:56:37.920 --> 00:56:39.560 align:middle line:84%
It's like, if you
see the infomercial,

00:56:39.560 --> 00:56:43.420 align:middle line:84%
you can then buy the product
and write down the phone number.

00:56:43.420 --> 00:56:43.920 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:56:43.920 --> 00:56:45.290 align:middle line:90%
Observations.

00:56:45.290 --> 00:56:47.720 align:middle line:84%
Again, this was one
of the early papers

00:56:47.720 --> 00:56:50.060 align:middle line:84%
that had an equilibrium
with price dispersion.

00:56:50.060 --> 00:56:52.770 align:middle line:84%
This is like the Spiegler
paper I talked about earlier.

00:56:52.770 --> 00:56:55.490 align:middle line:84%
This is like Stahl
from a few weeks ago.

00:56:55.490 --> 00:56:58.930 align:middle line:84%
Again, some consumers are
going to only get one ad.

00:56:58.930 --> 00:57:01.430 align:middle line:84%
If the consumer only got your
ad and nobody else's, then you

00:57:01.430 --> 00:57:05.840 align:middle line:84%
want to set p equals v, but
if they've got multiple ads,

00:57:05.840 --> 00:57:08.840 align:middle line:84%
we're again in this situation
where, in equilibrium, we're

00:57:08.840 --> 00:57:10.670 align:middle line:84%
going to get some
distribution on prices

00:57:10.670 --> 00:57:14.000 align:middle line:84%
that has an upper bound of v
and then goes down from there.

00:57:14.000 --> 00:57:17.600 align:middle line:84%
And it's just determined
by, I want to undercut,

00:57:17.600 --> 00:57:22.070 align:middle line:84%
but if they only see one ad,
I'd like to be the highest--

00:57:22.070 --> 00:57:24.590 align:middle line:84%
I'd like to set at price
equals v. But then they

00:57:24.590 --> 00:57:28.130 align:middle line:84%
might see two or three or four
or five ads, and so I undercut.

00:57:28.130 --> 00:57:30.660 align:middle line:84%
We get this mixed strategy
pricing equilibrium.

00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:33.950 align:middle line:90%


00:57:33.950 --> 00:57:36.260 align:middle line:90%
Advertising levels.

00:57:36.260 --> 00:57:41.310 align:middle line:84%
A prime of 0 is small and
A prime of 1 is really big.

00:57:41.310 --> 00:57:44.930 align:middle line:84%
So if I make it even more
stark, if this thing starts out

00:57:44.930 --> 00:57:47.660 align:middle line:84%
like in advertising, the
first people is really cheap,

00:57:47.660 --> 00:57:50.160 align:middle line:84%
and then reaching the last
people is really expensive,

00:57:50.160 --> 00:57:54.320 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be some
internal level x star

00:57:54.320 --> 00:57:56.360 align:middle line:90%
of advertising that I pick.

00:57:56.360 --> 00:57:58.340 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to
pick it x star so

00:57:58.340 --> 00:58:00.740 align:middle line:84%
that the marginal cost of
reaching one more consumer

00:58:00.740 --> 00:58:05.600 align:middle line:84%
equals the marginal profit I get
off the last consumer I reach.

00:58:05.600 --> 00:58:07.940 align:middle line:84%
So the first order
condition is going

00:58:07.940 --> 00:58:12.860 align:middle line:84%
to be the profit on the
marginal consumer reached

00:58:12.860 --> 00:58:16.080 align:middle line:84%
equals the cost of the
marginal consumer reached.

00:58:16.080 --> 00:58:18.700 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:58:18.700 --> 00:58:20.620 align:middle line:84%
Interesting observation
that Butters

00:58:20.620 --> 00:58:25.660 align:middle line:84%
has is that in this
model, advertising levels

00:58:25.660 --> 00:58:27.418 align:middle line:90%
are socially efficient.

00:58:27.418 --> 00:58:29.710 align:middle line:84%
You might have thought this
is like entry where there's

00:58:29.710 --> 00:58:31.900 align:middle line:84%
business stealing and
because of business stealing,

00:58:31.900 --> 00:58:33.880 align:middle line:90%
you could have too much entry.

00:58:33.880 --> 00:58:37.540 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be because you don't
internalize consumer surplus

00:58:37.540 --> 00:58:40.330 align:middle line:84%
gains, there could be too
little entry or entry being

00:58:40.330 --> 00:58:41.470 align:middle line:90%
advertising.

00:58:41.470 --> 00:58:44.170 align:middle line:84%
But in this model, with
the technologies he's got,

00:58:44.170 --> 00:58:46.610 align:middle line:84%
advertising is
socially efficient.

00:58:46.610 --> 00:58:48.160 align:middle line:90%
And we think of that like--

00:58:48.160 --> 00:58:51.190 align:middle line:84%
it's an argument that here's a
special case where there's not

00:58:51.190 --> 00:58:53.350 align:middle line:84%
a distortion, and the
informative advertising

00:58:53.350 --> 00:58:56.050 align:middle line:84%
in the world should exactly
match what a social planner

00:58:56.050 --> 00:58:57.820 align:middle line:90%
would want it to be.

00:58:57.820 --> 00:58:59.410 align:middle line:90%
And why is that?

00:58:59.410 --> 00:59:04.150 align:middle line:84%
So first order
condition for x star

00:59:04.150 --> 00:59:06.515 align:middle line:84%
gives the marginal cost
of reaching the consumer

00:59:06.515 --> 00:59:08.140 align:middle line:84%
equals the marginal
benefit to the firm

00:59:08.140 --> 00:59:10.150 align:middle line:90%
of reaching the consumer.

00:59:10.150 --> 00:59:14.290 align:middle line:84%
Marginal cost of reaching the
consumer is A prime of x star.

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:17.500 align:middle line:90%
What's the marginal benefit?

00:59:17.500 --> 00:59:20.510 align:middle line:84%
The marginal benefit,
I'm indifferent.

00:59:20.510 --> 00:59:21.980 align:middle line:90%
I'm mixing over these prices.

00:59:21.980 --> 00:59:24.190 align:middle line:84%
So I'm both choosing a
pure strategy x star,

00:59:24.190 --> 00:59:26.260 align:middle line:90%
and I'm mixing over prices.

00:59:26.260 --> 00:59:28.580 align:middle line:90%
So it must be I'm indifferent.

00:59:28.580 --> 00:59:31.750 align:middle line:84%
My profits are the same
regardless of which price

00:59:31.750 --> 00:59:33.830 align:middle line:84%
I choose from my
price distribution.

00:59:33.830 --> 00:59:35.740 align:middle line:84%
And so my profit on
the marginal consumer

00:59:35.740 --> 00:59:37.840 align:middle line:84%
is the same, regardless
of which profit price

00:59:37.840 --> 00:59:39.730 align:middle line:84%
I choose from the
price distribution.

00:59:39.730 --> 00:59:43.180 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to evaluate
it, looking at the case

00:59:43.180 --> 00:59:45.520 align:middle line:84%
where I set a price at the
upper bound of the price

00:59:45.520 --> 00:59:50.530 align:middle line:84%
distribution v. So what's the
marginal value of reaching

00:59:50.530 --> 00:59:53.140 align:middle line:84%
one more consumer when
I set a price of v?

00:59:53.140 --> 00:59:55.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, I get v minus
c is the profit

00:59:55.030 --> 00:59:56.920 align:middle line:90%
from selling to that consumer.

00:59:56.920 --> 01:00:01.840 align:middle line:84%
And then I get that
multiplied by the probability

01:00:01.840 --> 01:00:05.830 align:middle line:84%
that the consumer saw no other
ad, because when I set price v,

01:00:05.830 --> 01:00:09.010 align:middle line:84%
I sell to them only if they got
no other ad, because if they got

01:00:09.010 --> 01:00:10.930 align:middle line:84%
any other ad, they'll
find a lower price,

01:00:10.930 --> 01:00:13.120 align:middle line:90%
and they won't buy from me.

01:00:13.120 --> 01:00:18.800 align:middle line:84%
But this is the social
benefit of an ad,

01:00:18.800 --> 01:00:21.240 align:middle line:84%
of reaching a consumer who's
never been reached before.

01:00:21.240 --> 01:00:23.407 align:middle line:84%
Because if a consumer has
never been reached before,

01:00:23.407 --> 01:00:24.800 align:middle line:84%
the social surplus
from the firms

01:00:24.800 --> 01:00:30.950 align:middle line:84%
interacting with that consumer
goes from 0 to v minus c.

01:00:30.950 --> 01:00:37.610 align:middle line:84%
So what we're getting is
that the private return

01:00:37.610 --> 01:00:40.550 align:middle line:84%
to reach one more consumer
is the social return

01:00:40.550 --> 01:00:43.670 align:middle line:90%
to reaching one more consumer.

01:00:43.670 --> 01:00:46.220 align:middle line:84%
And it's not obvious if you
think about any other price.

01:00:46.220 --> 01:00:48.290 align:middle line:84%
When you set any
other price, you

01:00:48.290 --> 01:00:50.060 align:middle line:84%
are doing some
business stealing,

01:00:50.060 --> 01:00:53.612 align:middle line:84%
and you are stealing consumers
away from other firms,

01:00:53.612 --> 01:00:55.070 align:middle line:84%
and you are increasing
the consumer

01:00:55.070 --> 01:00:56.670 align:middle line:90%
surplus of the consumers.

01:00:56.670 --> 01:01:00.500 align:middle line:84%
But somehow, this model is such
that those two things do exactly

01:01:00.500 --> 01:01:01.790 align:middle line:90%
offset.

01:01:01.790 --> 01:01:05.605 align:middle line:84%
And so there's no social
inefficiency here.

01:01:05.605 --> 01:01:08.430 align:middle line:90%


01:01:08.430 --> 01:01:11.940 align:middle line:84%
So it's at least one case where
we don't worry about advertising

01:01:11.940 --> 01:01:14.640 align:middle line:90%
being distortionary.

01:01:14.640 --> 01:01:18.600 align:middle line:84%
And obviously,
this is an extreme.

01:01:18.600 --> 01:01:22.590 align:middle line:84%
Butters has come up with a
special case where this is true.

01:01:22.590 --> 01:01:28.680 align:middle line:84%
If you had the firm selling
imperfect substitutes rather

01:01:28.680 --> 01:01:32.970 align:middle line:84%
than perfect substitutes,
we wouldn't get this--

01:01:32.970 --> 01:01:36.430 align:middle line:84%
we could get this kind of
mixed strategy equilibrium.

01:01:36.430 --> 01:01:38.640 align:middle line:84%
We get the kind of pure
strategy equilibrium pricing

01:01:38.640 --> 01:01:41.070 align:middle line:84%
like we got with search,
with differentiated goods.

01:01:41.070 --> 01:01:42.810 align:middle line:84%
You'd have the business
stealing effects,

01:01:42.810 --> 01:01:45.130 align:middle line:84%
you'd have the consumer
surplus effects.

01:01:45.130 --> 01:01:47.125 align:middle line:84%
But I think, in the
entry literature,

01:01:47.125 --> 01:01:48.750 align:middle line:84%
we have this intuition
that one of them

01:01:48.750 --> 01:01:50.740 align:middle line:84%
was often going to be
bigger than the others.

01:01:50.740 --> 01:01:52.930 align:middle line:84%
Here, I think we don't
have that intuition.

01:01:52.930 --> 01:01:56.515 align:middle line:90%


01:01:56.515 --> 01:01:57.015 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:01:57.015 --> 01:02:00.170 align:middle line:90%


01:02:00.170 --> 01:02:02.600 align:middle line:84%
Second class of models I
want to talk about then

01:02:02.600 --> 01:02:06.080 align:middle line:84%
is, how do we think about
signaling models of advertising,

01:02:06.080 --> 01:02:09.410 align:middle line:90%
and when would they work?

01:02:09.410 --> 01:02:12.860 align:middle line:84%
So Milgrom and Roberts is
your classic paper doing this

01:02:12.860 --> 01:02:14.900 align:middle line:90%
in the 1980s.

01:02:14.900 --> 01:02:19.767 align:middle line:84%
Milgrom and Roberts'
model, all firms

01:02:19.767 --> 01:02:21.350 align:middle line:84%
that are entering
the market, they try

01:02:21.350 --> 01:02:22.790 align:middle line:90%
to come up with some product.

01:02:22.790 --> 01:02:25.540 align:middle line:90%
They invent some quality q.

01:02:25.540 --> 01:02:27.540 align:middle line:84%
We're just going to make
it a two-point support.

01:02:27.540 --> 01:02:31.430 align:middle line:84%
Your quality is
either low or high.

01:02:31.430 --> 01:02:33.800 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, all the firms are
trying to invent quality q,

01:02:33.800 --> 01:02:35.830 align:middle line:84%
but they sometimes
come up with quality H

01:02:35.830 --> 01:02:37.205 align:middle line:84%
or sometimes come
up with quality

01:02:37.205 --> 01:02:44.115 align:middle line:84%
L. There's some common prior
that the consumers and the firm

01:02:44.115 --> 01:02:45.990 align:middle line:84%
have over the probability
that the quality is

01:02:45.990 --> 01:02:48.090 align:middle line:90%
high of a new product.

01:02:48.090 --> 01:02:50.730 align:middle line:84%
The firm observes
its own quality

01:02:50.730 --> 01:02:54.390 align:middle line:84%
and then chooses a
price p for the product

01:02:54.390 --> 01:02:58.410 align:middle line:90%
and a level of advertising A.

01:02:58.410 --> 01:03:00.930 align:middle line:84%
Important in this
model is consumers

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:06.480 align:middle line:84%
observe A. The consumers observe
that they just put that sock

01:03:06.480 --> 01:03:07.630 align:middle line:90%
puppet on the Super Bowl.

01:03:07.630 --> 01:03:09.720 align:middle line:90%
That was $2.4 million.

01:03:09.720 --> 01:03:11.630 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, this
is what we tend

01:03:11.630 --> 01:03:14.040 align:middle line:84%
to refer to as a money
burning theory of advertising.

01:03:14.040 --> 01:03:17.970 align:middle line:84%
It almost doesn't matter
what they did with the money.

01:03:17.970 --> 01:03:21.600 align:middle line:84%
It's like, yeah, those guys just
put $2.4 million in dollar bills

01:03:21.600 --> 01:03:23.920 align:middle line:84%
in a big pile and
lit it on fire.

01:03:23.920 --> 01:03:27.450 align:middle line:84%
That proves to me
that they must have

01:03:27.450 --> 01:03:31.680 align:middle line:84%
thought it was worth it for
them to burn $2.4 million.

01:03:31.680 --> 01:03:33.690 align:middle line:84%
So they must be the
type of firm that wants

01:03:33.690 --> 01:03:36.720 align:middle line:90%
to make big bonfires of money.

01:03:36.720 --> 01:03:43.500 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, consumers observe
A. They Bayesian update

01:03:43.500 --> 01:03:47.040 align:middle line:84%
on what q is, knowing
what different firms would

01:03:47.040 --> 01:03:50.010 align:middle line:84%
do in terms of
burning money or not.

01:03:50.010 --> 01:03:53.190 align:middle line:84%
And so then they form a
posterior on what q is,

01:03:53.190 --> 01:03:55.440 align:middle line:90%
and they buy the product.

01:03:55.440 --> 01:03:57.023 align:middle line:84%
And then the thought
here is that this

01:03:57.023 --> 01:03:59.148 align:middle line:84%
is like a dynamic model,
although they don't really

01:03:59.148 --> 01:04:00.340 align:middle line:90%
model the dynamics fully.

01:04:00.340 --> 01:04:02.670 align:middle line:84%
But then after you
buy the product,

01:04:02.670 --> 01:04:05.370 align:middle line:84%
you learn something
more about the quality,

01:04:05.370 --> 01:04:07.590 align:middle line:84%
and then you may buy
the product again,

01:04:07.590 --> 01:04:09.300 align:middle line:84%
or you may buy it
a third time, you

01:04:09.300 --> 01:04:10.950 align:middle line:84%
may continue to buy
it for many years,

01:04:10.950 --> 01:04:13.680 align:middle line:84%
you may tell your friends
about it, whatever.

01:04:13.680 --> 01:04:17.350 align:middle line:84%
So you observe the A. You
decide whether to buy.

01:04:17.350 --> 01:04:19.950 align:middle line:84%
If you buy, you learn
something about the quality.

01:04:19.950 --> 01:04:24.040 align:middle line:84%
And then there's some repeat
purchasing going on later on.

01:04:24.040 --> 01:04:25.330 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:25.330 --> 01:04:28.780 align:middle line:84%
They say, whatever's going
on in all those later stages,

01:04:28.780 --> 01:04:31.760 align:middle line:84%
we're just going to model
this in a reduced form way.

01:04:31.760 --> 01:04:34.360 align:middle line:84%
We're going to say that
the profits the firm gets

01:04:34.360 --> 01:04:38.770 align:middle line:84%
are pi of p given
q and q hat that

01:04:38.770 --> 01:04:43.120 align:middle line:84%
depend both on the true quality
q and on the quality q hat

01:04:43.120 --> 01:04:46.310 align:middle line:84%
that consumers expect to receive
when making purchase decisions.

01:04:46.310 --> 01:04:49.450 align:middle line:84%
And generally, profit is
going to be increasing

01:04:49.450 --> 01:04:51.760 align:middle line:90%
in both of these arguments.

01:04:51.760 --> 01:04:53.823 align:middle line:84%
If consumers think
your product is good,

01:04:53.823 --> 01:04:55.240 align:middle line:84%
even though it's
not good, they're

01:04:55.240 --> 01:04:56.920 align:middle line:84%
still going to buy
it once, and you're

01:04:56.920 --> 01:04:59.170 align:middle line:84%
going to make money from
them thinking it's good.

01:04:59.170 --> 01:05:01.120 align:middle line:84%
But then conditional
on how good they

01:05:01.120 --> 01:05:03.320 align:middle line:84%
think it is, what makes
them buy it or not,

01:05:03.320 --> 01:05:05.327 align:middle line:84%
if it's actually good,
then when they buy it,

01:05:05.327 --> 01:05:06.410 align:middle line:90%
they're going to enjoy it.

01:05:06.410 --> 01:05:07.730 align:middle line:90%
They're going to buy it again.

01:05:07.730 --> 01:05:11.080 align:middle line:84%
And so your profit's increasing
in both the true quality q

01:05:11.080 --> 01:05:14.950 align:middle line:84%
and in q hat, which is
their posterior on q

01:05:14.950 --> 01:05:17.313 align:middle line:84%
after you've had your
advertising campaign

01:05:17.313 --> 01:05:18.105 align:middle line:90%
and set your price.

01:05:18.105 --> 01:05:24.990 align:middle line:90%


01:05:24.990 --> 01:05:26.320 align:middle line:90%
Observations.

01:05:26.320 --> 01:05:29.040 align:middle line:84%
This model can have a
separating equilibrium,

01:05:29.040 --> 01:05:33.570 align:middle line:84%
where only firms with
high quality advertise.

01:05:33.570 --> 01:05:36.240 align:middle line:84%
So we get this
equilibrium where you

01:05:36.240 --> 01:05:40.560 align:middle line:84%
have these two things, a low
price and zero advertising

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:44.220 align:middle line:84%
and a high price and some
level of advertising.

01:05:44.220 --> 01:05:46.690 align:middle line:84%
And all the low
quality firms do this.

01:05:46.690 --> 01:05:48.650 align:middle line:84%
All the high quality
firms do this.

01:05:48.650 --> 01:05:53.790 align:middle line:90%


01:05:53.790 --> 01:05:56.790 align:middle line:90%
When is it that this can happen?

01:05:56.790 --> 01:05:59.380 align:middle line:84%
I think two things you're
going to want to have.

01:05:59.380 --> 01:06:03.900 align:middle line:84%
First is that repeat purchases
have to be important.

01:06:03.900 --> 01:06:06.200 align:middle line:84%
If there were no repeat
purchases in the model,

01:06:06.200 --> 01:06:11.240 align:middle line:84%
this wouldn't happen because if
there were no repeat purchases,

01:06:11.240 --> 01:06:14.900 align:middle line:84%
low and high quality firms would
earn exactly the same profits.

01:06:14.900 --> 01:06:16.910 align:middle line:84%
So if the high
quality firms found

01:06:16.910 --> 01:06:19.430 align:middle line:84%
it better to set a high
price and burn money,

01:06:19.430 --> 01:06:22.580 align:middle line:84%
the low quality firms would
also set a high price,

01:06:22.580 --> 01:06:23.743 align:middle line:90%
profit, and burn money.

01:06:23.743 --> 01:06:25.160 align:middle line:84%
So what you have
to have happen is

01:06:25.160 --> 01:06:26.577 align:middle line:84%
there's got to be
repeat purchases

01:06:26.577 --> 01:06:28.635 align:middle line:84%
so that the high
quality firms are

01:06:28.635 --> 01:06:30.260 align:middle line:84%
willing to burn this
money because they

01:06:30.260 --> 01:06:32.610 align:middle line:84%
know the consumers
will keep coming back.

01:06:32.610 --> 01:06:35.270 align:middle line:84%
The low quality firms are only
going to sell to consumers once.

01:06:35.270 --> 01:06:39.770 align:middle line:84%
So you have to burn an amount
of money such that the--

01:06:39.770 --> 01:06:45.680 align:middle line:84%
it's got to be that
pH minus pL or pH

01:06:45.680 --> 01:06:54.740 align:middle line:84%
minus c D of pH or D1
of pH minus pH minus c--

01:06:54.740 --> 01:06:55.900 align:middle line:90%
pL minus c.

01:06:55.900 --> 01:06:58.760 align:middle line:90%


01:06:58.760 --> 01:07:01.650 align:middle line:84%
So imagine that this
is the extra profits

01:07:01.650 --> 01:07:05.650 align:middle line:84%
I get in the first period
from burning the money.

01:07:05.650 --> 01:07:09.120 align:middle line:84%
It's got to be that
this is less than A

01:07:09.120 --> 01:07:12.558 align:middle line:84%
because if this is less-- if
this was bigger than A, then

01:07:12.558 --> 01:07:14.100 align:middle line:84%
even the low quality
firms would just

01:07:14.100 --> 01:07:15.600 align:middle line:84%
go ahead and burn
the money in order

01:07:15.600 --> 01:07:18.120 align:middle line:84%
to get the extra demand
in the first period.

01:07:18.120 --> 01:07:20.820 align:middle line:84%
So it's got to be that
this is less than A,

01:07:20.820 --> 01:07:23.100 align:middle line:84%
but this plus the long-run
benefit that you get

01:07:23.100 --> 01:07:26.640 align:middle line:84%
from the repeat purchases
at t equals 2 or t equals 3

01:07:26.640 --> 01:07:29.500 align:middle line:84%
or whatever does make
it worth advertising.

01:07:29.500 --> 01:07:33.370 align:middle line:84%
And so we need a repeat
purchasing effect.

01:07:33.370 --> 01:07:37.260 align:middle line:84%
And then we also need
some kind of small--

01:07:37.260 --> 01:07:39.420 align:middle line:84%
we need word of mouth
learning effects

01:07:39.420 --> 01:07:41.947 align:middle line:84%
to be small because if word
of mouth learning effects

01:07:41.947 --> 01:07:43.530 align:middle line:84%
were going to be
small, you could just

01:07:43.530 --> 01:07:50.910 align:middle line:84%
come in, set a price of
pH, do zero advertising,

01:07:50.910 --> 01:07:52.800 align:middle line:84%
have only a few people
buy your product.

01:07:52.800 --> 01:07:54.300 align:middle line:84%
Then those few
people tell everybody

01:07:54.300 --> 01:07:56.300 align:middle line:84%
and they tell everybody
and they tell everybody,

01:07:56.300 --> 01:07:59.520 align:middle line:84%
and the thing takes off, and
the long-run benefits are there,

01:07:59.520 --> 01:08:01.770 align:middle line:84%
even without having spent
the money on the advertising

01:08:01.770 --> 01:08:03.100 align:middle line:90%
in the first place.

01:08:03.100 --> 01:08:09.150 align:middle line:84%
So what we need is something
about repeat purchases help--

01:08:09.150 --> 01:08:11.910 align:middle line:84%
getting people to buy
once, the repeat purchases

01:08:11.910 --> 01:08:14.110 align:middle line:84%
make it worth spending
the advertising.

01:08:14.110 --> 01:08:16.470 align:middle line:84%
And you can't do it without
spending the advertising

01:08:16.470 --> 01:08:19.000 align:middle line:84%
because you can't do it
by just a small release

01:08:19.000 --> 01:08:21.725 align:middle line:84%
and then let people tell
their friends instead.

01:08:21.725 --> 01:08:26.510 align:middle line:90%


01:08:26.510 --> 01:08:29.630 align:middle line:84%
Technologically, this
advertising is money burning.

01:08:29.630 --> 01:08:32.630 align:middle line:84%
It's not changing the
utility functions at all,

01:08:32.630 --> 01:08:36.979 align:middle line:84%
but it can increase-- it can
be socially valuable if it

01:08:36.979 --> 01:08:38.790 align:middle line:90%
increases the match quality.

01:08:38.790 --> 01:08:41.600 align:middle line:84%
So if you imagine a
model where you have--

01:08:41.600 --> 01:08:43.370 align:middle line:84%
there are some people
who only really

01:08:43.370 --> 01:08:45.319 align:middle line:90%
have value for a high quality--

01:08:45.319 --> 01:08:46.979 align:middle line:84%
some people who
are serious hikers.

01:08:46.979 --> 01:08:49.340 align:middle line:84%
And so if it's not a really
good Patagonia coat that's

01:08:49.340 --> 01:08:51.260 align:middle line:84%
actually going to keep
you warm on a mountain,

01:08:51.260 --> 01:08:52.196 align:middle line:90%
you don't want it.

01:08:52.196 --> 01:08:54.529 align:middle line:84%
And then there's some low
quality consumers who are just

01:08:54.529 --> 01:08:55.830 align:middle line:90%
wearing it around town.

01:08:55.830 --> 01:08:58.502 align:middle line:84%
And if it doesn't
actually prevent the wind

01:08:58.502 --> 01:08:59.960 align:middle line:84%
from getting you,
it doesn't really

01:08:59.960 --> 01:09:02.210 align:middle line:84%
matter because you're just
wearing it around the city.

01:09:02.210 --> 01:09:05.870 align:middle line:84%
If the advertising helps
us with the match quality

01:09:05.870 --> 01:09:08.930 align:middle line:84%
and lets the people who
want high quality buy

01:09:08.930 --> 01:09:11.750 align:middle line:84%
high quality, people who want
low quality, buy low quality,

01:09:11.750 --> 01:09:14.810 align:middle line:84%
then this sort of money
burning can raise utility.

01:09:14.810 --> 01:09:17.660 align:middle line:84%
And we might not want to
ban it, even though it's not

01:09:17.660 --> 01:09:19.800 align:middle line:90%
technologically valuable.

01:09:19.800 --> 01:09:24.060 align:middle line:84%
But it can be the only way
to signal information here.

01:09:24.060 --> 01:09:27.920 align:middle line:90%


01:09:27.920 --> 01:09:32.460 align:middle line:84%
And in some models
like this, you

01:09:32.460 --> 01:09:36.609 align:middle line:84%
can actually even do signaling
without any advertising at all.

01:09:36.609 --> 01:09:39.330 align:middle line:84%
So it's possible
that there can be--

01:09:39.330 --> 01:09:40.529 align:middle line:90%
don't go in advertising.

01:09:40.529 --> 01:09:44.010 align:middle line:84%
There can be equilibria like
this, where some firms set

01:09:44.010 --> 01:09:47.340 align:middle line:84%
a really high price,
and then people

01:09:47.340 --> 01:09:50.550 align:middle line:84%
know that they're
only going to sell

01:09:50.550 --> 01:09:51.760 align:middle line:90%
to a small number of people.

01:09:51.760 --> 01:09:53.040 align:middle line:84%
So it must be they're
expecting to sell

01:09:53.040 --> 01:09:55.613 align:middle line:84%
to that small number of people
over and over and over again.

01:09:55.613 --> 01:09:57.280 align:middle line:84%
Whereas you can set
a low price and sell

01:09:57.280 --> 01:10:01.210 align:middle line:84%
to a lot of people who just buy
once or something like that.

01:10:01.210 --> 01:10:05.580 align:middle line:84%
So you can get you can
get price as a signal.

01:10:05.580 --> 01:10:08.170 align:middle line:84%
You can get price as a signal
even without advertising.

01:10:08.170 --> 01:10:11.820 align:middle line:84%
But advertising makes it
easier to get signaling models

01:10:11.820 --> 01:10:14.370 align:middle line:90%
to work out.

01:10:14.370 --> 01:10:14.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:10:14.870 --> 01:10:20.270 align:middle line:90%


01:10:20.270 --> 01:10:20.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:10:20.870 --> 01:10:21.680 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

01:10:21.680 --> 01:10:23.960 align:middle line:84%
So then I thought
the final one, I

01:10:23.960 --> 01:10:27.440 align:middle line:84%
will go to things
based on my beer ad.

01:10:27.440 --> 01:10:31.220 align:middle line:84%
And here, I'm covering an
unpublished paper that's

01:10:31.220 --> 01:10:34.110 align:middle line:84%
unpublished for 15
years or whatever.

01:10:34.110 --> 01:10:37.010 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure Jesse has long
since given up on this paper,

01:10:37.010 --> 01:10:39.560 align:middle line:84%
but I always really
liked it, so I'm going

01:10:39.560 --> 01:10:41.010 align:middle line:90%
to keep covering it anyway.

01:10:41.010 --> 01:10:44.960 align:middle line:84%
So this is a behavioral
economics paper

01:10:44.960 --> 01:10:47.940 align:middle line:84%
about advertising
changing preferences.

01:10:47.940 --> 01:10:49.850 align:middle line:84%
And it starts out
with noting, there's

01:10:49.850 --> 01:10:55.010 align:middle line:84%
a lot of very, very good
evidence about fallibility

01:10:55.010 --> 01:10:57.890 align:middle line:84%
of memory and
manipulability of memory

01:10:57.890 --> 01:11:00.210 align:middle line:84%
that makes you think that
these things can work.

01:11:00.210 --> 01:11:04.370 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, one of these
very classic experiments,

01:11:04.370 --> 01:11:09.590 align:middle line:84%
the famous person experiment
is, I give you a list of people.

01:11:09.590 --> 01:11:14.330 align:middle line:84%
And I write down, I give you a
list of kind of famous people,

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:17.880 align:middle line:84%
and I ask you, which of
these people are famous?

01:11:17.880 --> 01:11:21.510 align:middle line:84%
And I put in a bunch of names
on there like, Ban Ki-moon

01:11:21.510 --> 01:11:23.500 align:middle line:84%
and Sebastian
Weisdorf and whatever.

01:11:23.500 --> 01:11:26.485 align:middle line:84%
And these people are people
who you've kind of heard of.

01:11:26.485 --> 01:11:28.110 align:middle line:84%
And you're like, OK,
yeah, Ban Ki-moon,

01:11:28.110 --> 01:11:31.710 align:middle line:84%
that's some guy who
did something UN

01:11:31.710 --> 01:11:32.715 align:middle line:90%
official or something.

01:11:32.715 --> 01:11:34.090 align:middle line:84%
I don't remember
quite who he is.

01:11:34.090 --> 01:11:37.240 align:middle line:84%
Sebastian Weisdorf, that
just doesn't ring a bell.

01:11:37.240 --> 01:11:38.430 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to put not famous.

01:11:38.430 --> 01:11:41.220 align:middle line:84%
And you put names of
actresses and whatever

01:11:41.220 --> 01:11:44.370 align:middle line:84%
that, again, you know, I have
no idea what that person's ever

01:11:44.370 --> 01:11:47.430 align:middle line:84%
been in, but I know that's
the name of some actress

01:11:47.430 --> 01:11:48.257 align:middle line:90%
and whatever.

01:11:48.257 --> 01:11:50.340 align:middle line:84%
And people are pretty good
at this task of saying,

01:11:50.340 --> 01:11:51.155 align:middle line:90%
is someone famous?

01:11:51.155 --> 01:11:52.530 align:middle line:84%
And one of the
interesting things

01:11:52.530 --> 01:11:55.210 align:middle line:84%
is you give people that task,
you give them, here's 20 people,

01:11:55.210 --> 01:11:56.980 align:middle line:90%
is this person famous or not?

01:11:56.980 --> 01:11:58.740 align:middle line:84%
And some of them are
just made up names.

01:11:58.740 --> 01:12:00.430 align:middle line:84%
And people are very,
very good at that task.

01:12:00.430 --> 01:12:02.730 align:middle line:84%
They can write down famous,
famous, not famous, famous,

01:12:02.730 --> 01:12:04.605 align:middle line:84%
not famous, even though
they have no idea who

01:12:04.605 --> 01:12:06.630 align:middle line:90%
the people actually are.

01:12:06.630 --> 01:12:08.970 align:middle line:84%
And then you bring the same
students back in the list--

01:12:08.970 --> 01:12:10.240 align:middle line:90%
into the lab.

01:12:10.240 --> 01:12:13.680 align:middle line:84%
One week later, you hand
them exactly the same list

01:12:13.680 --> 01:12:15.600 align:middle line:90%
that they said yes or no on.

01:12:15.600 --> 01:12:18.180 align:middle line:84%
And they're much
worse at the task.

01:12:18.180 --> 01:12:20.820 align:middle line:84%
And it's because, OK,
Sebastian Weisdorf,

01:12:20.820 --> 01:12:22.500 align:middle line:90%
I've kind of heard that.

01:12:22.500 --> 01:12:24.120 align:middle line:84%
But is that because
Sebastian Weisdorf

01:12:24.120 --> 01:12:25.578 align:middle line:84%
is some guy I've
heard of, or is it

01:12:25.578 --> 01:12:28.960 align:middle line:84%
because he was a guy who was on
the list that I did last week?

01:12:28.960 --> 01:12:31.680 align:middle line:84%
And people no longer
can remember the things

01:12:31.680 --> 01:12:34.350 align:middle line:84%
as well as they did the
week before or whatever.

01:12:34.350 --> 01:12:37.350 align:middle line:84%
He also gives these
examples of these marketing

01:12:37.350 --> 01:12:39.808 align:middle line:84%
experiments with
orange juice, where you

01:12:39.808 --> 01:12:41.100 align:middle line:90%
have people taste orange juice.

01:12:41.100 --> 01:12:43.500 align:middle line:84%
And you prime them before
with the correct answers,

01:12:43.500 --> 01:12:49.650 align:middle line:84%
like here's an orange juice
that's got a really tangy taste

01:12:49.650 --> 01:12:50.415 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

01:12:50.415 --> 01:12:53.040 align:middle line:84%
And then here's an orange juice
that's got a really sweet taste

01:12:53.040 --> 01:12:53.520 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

01:12:53.520 --> 01:12:54.437 align:middle line:90%
Which one do you like?

01:12:54.437 --> 01:12:58.920 align:middle line:84%
And then the tangy
taste one is just like,

01:12:58.920 --> 01:13:00.420 align:middle line:84%
you put vinegar in
the orange juice,

01:13:00.420 --> 01:13:01.808 align:middle line:90%
and you just made it worse.

01:13:01.808 --> 01:13:03.600 align:middle line:84%
But if you've described
it like, oh, here's

01:13:03.600 --> 01:13:04.950 align:middle line:84%
a really sour
orange juice taste,

01:13:04.950 --> 01:13:06.570 align:middle line:84%
or here's a really tangy
one taste, somehow,

01:13:06.570 --> 01:13:08.130 align:middle line:84%
if you've told them
it's a really tangy one,

01:13:08.130 --> 01:13:10.140 align:middle line:84%
they're like, oh, yeah, I
like that one, that's tangy,

01:13:10.140 --> 01:13:11.260 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

01:13:11.260 --> 01:13:15.390 align:middle line:84%
And so his thought on
the beer example is,

01:13:15.390 --> 01:13:18.150 align:middle line:84%
people have had beer many
times in their lives,

01:13:18.150 --> 01:13:20.980 align:middle line:84%
and they've had parties with
their friends many times.

01:13:20.980 --> 01:13:24.720 align:middle line:84%
And the thing is, you
like to go to events where

01:13:24.720 --> 01:13:26.400 align:middle line:90%
you're having a good time.

01:13:26.400 --> 01:13:28.980 align:middle line:84%
And you don't remember
whether it was me

01:13:28.980 --> 01:13:30.740 align:middle line:84%
who had a good time
drinking the Budweiser

01:13:30.740 --> 01:13:34.740 align:middle line:84%
or if it was those people I
saw on TV having a good time

01:13:34.740 --> 01:13:36.010 align:middle line:90%
drinking the Budweiser.

01:13:36.010 --> 01:13:37.290 align:middle line:84%
And so then when I'm
there in the store,

01:13:37.290 --> 01:13:38.707 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to
remember, oh, should

01:13:38.707 --> 01:13:39.858 align:middle line:90%
I buy the Budweiser or not?

01:13:39.858 --> 01:13:42.150 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just trying to remember
some previous time when

01:13:42.150 --> 01:13:43.720 align:middle line:84%
I had a party and
I served Budweiser,

01:13:43.720 --> 01:13:45.250 align:middle line:90%
did people have a lot of fun?

01:13:45.250 --> 01:13:47.140 align:middle line:84%
And I can't keep
track of whether it

01:13:47.140 --> 01:13:49.810 align:middle line:84%
was my party with the Budweiser
where people had the fun

01:13:49.810 --> 01:13:52.840 align:middle line:84%
or it was that one on
TV I saw with people

01:13:52.840 --> 01:13:54.980 align:middle line:84%
having Budweiser, where
everyone had a great time.

01:13:54.980 --> 01:13:57.760 align:middle line:84%
And in some sense,
they've changed my memory

01:13:57.760 --> 01:13:59.593 align:middle line:84%
and put all these
memories in there

01:13:59.593 --> 01:14:01.510 align:middle line:84%
that I can't distinguish
from my own memories.

01:14:01.510 --> 01:14:03.370 align:middle line:90%
And therefore I can't--

01:14:03.370 --> 01:14:05.860 align:middle line:84%
and so you're changing
my preferences

01:14:05.860 --> 01:14:10.330 align:middle line:84%
by changing what I remember
about my prior experiences

01:14:10.330 --> 01:14:12.820 align:middle line:84%
with the product,
confusing my experiences

01:14:12.820 --> 01:14:15.640 align:middle line:84%
with the people on TV's
experiences with the product.

01:14:15.640 --> 01:14:19.990 align:middle line:84%
So the way he does this is he
has this continuum of consumers

01:14:19.990 --> 01:14:22.030 align:middle line:90%
who have types theta i.

01:14:22.030 --> 01:14:26.140 align:middle line:84%
And the type theta consumers get
benefit v with probability theta

01:14:26.140 --> 01:14:27.620 align:middle line:90%
when they consume the good.

01:14:27.620 --> 01:14:30.100 align:middle line:84%
So could this just be--
depending on your friend group,

01:14:30.100 --> 01:14:31.600 align:middle line:84%
you could be in a
friend group where

01:14:31.600 --> 01:14:35.260 align:middle line:84%
serving Budweiser at your party,
100% chance the party goes well.

01:14:35.260 --> 01:14:37.510 align:middle line:84%
You could be in a friend
group where serving Budweiser

01:14:37.510 --> 01:14:40.210 align:middle line:84%
at your party, just 0%
chance the party goes well

01:14:40.210 --> 01:14:42.940 align:middle line:84%
because your friends
all hate Budweiser.

01:14:42.940 --> 01:14:43.690 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

01:14:43.690 --> 01:14:47.290 align:middle line:84%
So each consumer is different
in their experiences

01:14:47.290 --> 01:14:50.020 align:middle line:90%
when they've consumed the good.

01:14:50.020 --> 01:14:54.700 align:middle line:84%
Consumers have consumed the
good on N prior occasions

01:14:54.700 --> 01:14:56.290 align:middle line:90%
and gotten these draws.

01:14:56.290 --> 01:14:59.120 align:middle line:90%
But they don't know theta.

01:14:59.120 --> 01:15:01.750 align:middle line:84%
I can't go, every
time in my life

01:15:01.750 --> 01:15:04.610 align:middle line:84%
that I've bought Budweiser for
a party, how did that party go?

01:15:04.610 --> 01:15:06.830 align:middle line:84%
Let me count them all
up and add them all up.

01:15:06.830 --> 01:15:09.820 align:middle line:84%
What I do is I just
remember often just one.

01:15:09.820 --> 01:15:11.270 align:middle line:90%
I just call to mind.

01:15:11.270 --> 01:15:14.090 align:middle line:84%
Let me try to remember some
time when I served Budweiser.

01:15:14.090 --> 01:15:15.830 align:middle line:90%
I remember that experience.

01:15:15.830 --> 01:15:17.320 align:middle line:90%
I remember how it went.

01:15:17.320 --> 01:15:20.770 align:middle line:90%
And then I Bayesian update.

01:15:20.770 --> 01:15:24.910 align:middle line:84%
So I have some-- there's some
common prior over the thetas.

01:15:24.910 --> 01:15:26.530 align:middle line:84%
And then I Bayesian
update to form,

01:15:26.530 --> 01:15:30.820 align:middle line:84%
what's my expected view of my
preference for the product,

01:15:30.820 --> 01:15:34.630 align:middle line:84%
conditional on the events
that I'm remembering?

01:15:34.630 --> 01:15:37.510 align:middle line:84%
And then what Jesse does,
he models advertising

01:15:37.510 --> 01:15:42.700 align:middle line:84%
as potentially altering
memory in one of two ways.

01:15:42.700 --> 01:15:47.560 align:middle line:84%
One is an ad could just take
a memory out of your head

01:15:47.560 --> 01:15:50.110 align:middle line:84%
and stick a fake
memory in instead.

01:15:50.110 --> 01:15:52.420 align:middle line:84%
So it's just taking one
of your N observations

01:15:52.420 --> 01:15:54.390 align:middle line:84%
out, putting another
observation in

01:15:54.390 --> 01:15:56.140 align:middle line:84%
so that when you do
the Bayesian updating,

01:15:56.140 --> 01:15:59.830 align:middle line:90%
you might recall that memory.

01:15:59.830 --> 01:16:04.550 align:middle line:84%
The other thing is a
notion of priming memories.

01:16:04.550 --> 01:16:08.770 align:middle line:84%
That is, we all have all
these memories in our heads.

01:16:08.770 --> 01:16:12.430 align:middle line:84%
If I get you to make one
memory, call that memory

01:16:12.430 --> 01:16:14.305 align:middle line:84%
to the front of your
mind, then the next time

01:16:14.305 --> 01:16:16.680 align:middle line:84%
you're trying to call a memory
to the front of your mind,

01:16:16.680 --> 01:16:18.190 align:middle line:84%
that one will come
forward again.

01:16:18.190 --> 01:16:21.310 align:middle line:84%
It's been more recent and
more primed to come forward.

01:16:21.310 --> 01:16:25.660 align:middle line:84%
And so here, this is, you show
people a positive experience

01:16:25.660 --> 01:16:27.010 align:middle line:90%
while they're watching the ad.

01:16:27.010 --> 01:16:29.920 align:middle line:84%
They remember the most similar
experience that they've had.

01:16:29.920 --> 01:16:31.420 align:middle line:84%
And then because
that experience has

01:16:31.420 --> 01:16:34.490 align:middle line:84%
been brought through to
the front of their mind,

01:16:34.490 --> 01:16:37.500 align:middle line:84%
it's more likely to be drawn
the next time it comes out.

01:16:37.500 --> 01:16:40.640 align:middle line:84%
So this is like you're
manipulating memories

01:16:40.640 --> 01:16:44.397 align:middle line:84%
by making people's
memories more--

01:16:44.397 --> 01:16:46.730 align:middle line:84%
changing the probability of
recall of different memories

01:16:46.730 --> 01:16:48.500 align:middle line:90%
that they have.

01:16:48.500 --> 01:16:51.890 align:middle line:84%
And then the paper itself,
it mostly focuses on this.

01:16:51.890 --> 01:16:57.590 align:middle line:84%
It gets into this highly
rational case where consumers

01:16:57.590 --> 01:17:00.620 align:middle line:84%
are aware that their memories
are being manipulated,

01:17:00.620 --> 01:17:02.840 align:middle line:84%
and they are aware
of the equilibrium

01:17:02.840 --> 01:17:06.510 align:middle line:84%
level of memory manipulation
that's going on by the firms.

01:17:06.510 --> 01:17:09.000 align:middle line:90%
And then they try to actually--

01:17:09.000 --> 01:17:11.412 align:middle line:90%


01:17:11.412 --> 01:17:12.870 align:middle line:84%
if I were him, I
might have started

01:17:12.870 --> 01:17:15.515 align:middle line:84%
with more of the non-fully
rational thing, where

01:17:15.515 --> 01:17:16.890 align:middle line:84%
people are just
being manipulated

01:17:16.890 --> 01:17:17.765 align:middle line:90%
and don't realize it.

01:17:17.765 --> 01:17:19.710 align:middle line:84%
But he's mostly
doing the, they are

01:17:19.710 --> 01:17:22.570 align:middle line:84%
trying to offset the
manipulation of their memories.

01:17:22.570 --> 01:17:25.060 align:middle line:84%
And so, in some sense,
they downweight it.

01:17:25.060 --> 01:17:27.710 align:middle line:84%
They're like, I know this firm
in equilibrium is advertising.

01:17:27.710 --> 01:17:30.210 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, I know that this
memory might be mine or might be

01:17:30.210 --> 01:17:31.350 align:middle line:90%
someone else's.

01:17:31.350 --> 01:17:33.690 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, when I'm doing
the Bayesian updating,

01:17:33.690 --> 01:17:36.750 align:middle line:84%
I'm doing the Bayesian updating
of expectation of theta

01:17:36.750 --> 01:17:39.318 align:middle line:84%
given ri and given
A star, where I

01:17:39.318 --> 01:17:40.860 align:middle line:84%
know what the
equilibrium advertising

01:17:40.860 --> 01:17:42.190 align:middle line:90%
level is in the world.

01:17:42.190 --> 01:17:46.230 align:middle line:84%
And so I know, and I'm
trying to undo this.

01:17:46.230 --> 01:17:49.980 align:middle line:84%
But nonetheless, the point
is, even in this model,

01:17:49.980 --> 01:17:52.860 align:middle line:84%
where memories are being
artificially manipulated,

01:17:52.860 --> 01:17:56.010 align:middle line:84%
firms might still want to
manipulate the memories.

01:17:56.010 --> 01:18:00.720 align:middle line:84%
And this is kind of like for
information design reasons.

01:18:00.720 --> 01:18:04.360 align:middle line:84%
So you're not
changing the mean--

01:18:04.360 --> 01:18:07.360 align:middle line:84%
you're not changing the mean
level of people's posterior

01:18:07.360 --> 01:18:11.710 align:middle line:84%
on theta, but you are changing
the distributions on the theta.

01:18:11.710 --> 01:18:13.870 align:middle line:84%
And so if you can change
the distribution of thetas

01:18:13.870 --> 01:18:17.200 align:middle line:84%
in a way that lets you extract
more of the social surplus,

01:18:17.200 --> 01:18:21.070 align:middle line:84%
it may make sense to advertise
even if the advertising is

01:18:21.070 --> 01:18:22.190 align:middle line:90%
being undone.

01:18:22.190 --> 01:18:24.040 align:middle line:84%
So if the advertising
is not being undone,

01:18:24.040 --> 01:18:25.820 align:middle line:84%
it's clear you
want to advertise.

01:18:25.820 --> 01:18:27.490 align:middle line:84%
But even when it's
being undone, you

01:18:27.490 --> 01:18:30.610 align:middle line:84%
might want to do it
for information design

01:18:30.610 --> 01:18:32.350 align:middle line:84%
reasons, where if
I can get everyone

01:18:32.350 --> 01:18:36.040 align:middle line:84%
to have the same posterior,
then I can charge that posterior

01:18:36.040 --> 01:18:37.940 align:middle line:84%
and extract all
the social surplus.

01:18:37.940 --> 01:18:39.640 align:middle line:84%
Or if there's more
heterogeneity,

01:18:39.640 --> 01:18:42.010 align:middle line:84%
I only extract a fraction
of the social surplus.

01:18:42.010 --> 01:18:44.530 align:middle line:90%


01:18:44.530 --> 01:18:45.140 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

01:18:45.140 --> 01:18:47.780 align:middle line:84%
So it's just got some
interesting observations.

01:18:47.780 --> 01:18:49.360 align:middle line:84%
And it's also
interesting about--

01:18:49.360 --> 01:18:51.760 align:middle line:84%
you can think about
when advertising

01:18:51.760 --> 01:18:53.530 align:middle line:84%
is going to be more
and less likely

01:18:53.530 --> 01:18:58.143 align:middle line:84%
because it's going to
depend on product quality

01:18:58.143 --> 01:19:00.310 align:middle line:84%
because if you actually
have a high-quality product,

01:19:00.310 --> 01:19:02.602 align:middle line:84%
you don't need-- you don't
get as much marginal benefit

01:19:02.602 --> 01:19:04.630 align:middle line:84%
from replacing memories
in people's minds

01:19:04.630 --> 01:19:07.190 align:middle line:84%
with fake memories because
the true memories are good.

01:19:07.190 --> 01:19:09.610 align:middle line:84%
And so you can get this
sort of advertising

01:19:09.610 --> 01:19:12.690 align:middle line:84%
as a signal of bad quality and
various interesting things.

01:19:12.690 --> 01:19:16.460 align:middle line:90%


01:19:16.460 --> 01:19:17.180 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:19:17.180 --> 01:19:18.600 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, I am out of time.

01:19:18.600 --> 01:19:22.070 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, Monday,
I'm going to-- again,

01:19:22.070 --> 01:19:23.820 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to do auctions.

01:19:23.820 --> 01:19:25.650 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
mostly auction theory.

01:19:25.650 --> 01:19:28.080 align:middle line:84%
I am going to do one empirical
paper that I really like.

01:19:28.080 --> 01:19:33.260 align:middle line:84%
This is the Hendricks and
Porter paper about offshore oil

01:19:33.260 --> 01:19:35.180 align:middle line:90%
drilling auctions.

01:19:35.180 --> 01:19:37.160 align:middle line:84%
And then, yeah, that's
the plan for next week.

01:19:37.160 --> 01:19:38.580 align:middle line:90%
I'll do auctions.

01:19:38.580 --> 01:19:41.480 align:middle line:84%
Tobias comes in
Wednesday and does

01:19:41.480 --> 01:19:43.040 align:middle line:90%
structural empirical auctions.

01:19:43.040 --> 01:19:46.330 align:middle line:84%
Then we go back to advertising
empirics the week after.

01:19:46.330 --> 01:19:58.000 align:middle line:90%